      {
        "identifier": "https://openalex.org/W4313787567",
        "title": "Corsairs Unbowed: Naples & Tripoli 1828",
        "creator": [
          "JOHN PAUL WRIGHT"
        ],
        "archival_reference": null,
        "specialist_agent": "contradiction",
        "description": "The Maghreb Review, Vol. 43, 1, 2018 © The Maghreb Review 2018 This publication is printed on FSC Mix paper from responsible sources CORSAIRS UNBOWED: NAPLES & TRIPOLI 1828 JOHN WRIGHT* Introduction At dawn on 22 August 1828 a fleet of about twenty warships appeared off the port of Tripoli, one of the so-called “Nests of Barbary Corsairs”. Its clear, aggressive intention was to enforce a favourable settlement by the Kingdom of the Two Sicilies (Naples) in its current diplomatic dispute with Yusuf Pasha Karamanli, ruler of the independent Regency of Tripoli. The fleet’s unannounced arrival prompted two days of urgent and complex shuttle diplomacy between the Neapolitan flagship, the Pasha’s Castle (the seat of government) and the Consulates of Great Britain, France, Tuscany and Naples. After two days, and two hours before sunset, when these diplomatic efforts had clearly failed, the fleet opened fire, causing little or no damage to its main target, the Pasha’s Castle, or the town, the land and sea fortifications, or the Tripoline corsair fleet still riding at anchor just outside the large, natural harbour. During the next two days of strong north-easterly gales (August 24 and 25) the Neapolitan fleet was inactive. But even when the weather from August 26 allowed three fine days for bombardment by the fleet’s three or four large gunboats, their exploding bombs (although undoubtedly alarming) caused but slight damage. The other warships failed to come effectively into action with massive broadsides firing heavy round shot, probably because they were too far out to sea, and thus out of range. Then, early on 28 August, nearly a week after its arrival, and with no discernible diplomatic or military achievement, the fleet, “to the great surprise of all”, was seen sailing off northwards, never to return. Indeed, no foreign war fleet was to assault Tripoli for another 83 years, until in October 1911 the Kingdom of Italy invaded this last Ottoman Turkish possession in North Africa.1 These events of August 1828 were significant for several reasons. Here was the very last of a series of intermittent European naval assaults going back nearly 200 years on Tripoli and other Barbary corsair bases (apart from the final French assault on Algiers in 1830). Even if this last attack on Tripoli failed ignominiously, the general ending of hostilities confirmed the overall, postNapoleonic predominance of the main European powers (Britain and France in * Richmond, UK 1 Contemporary sources for the 1828 assault include National Archives, Kew, London: Foreign Office Papers: FO 76/23, British Consul General, Hanmer Warrington, to Secretary of State, Colonial Department, Sir George Murray. 5th September & 28th October 1828; to R.W.Hay, Colonial Department, 31st December 1828. enclosed, Diary, July-December 1828. Maria José Vilar, Un Diario Español inédito de la expedition de la Real Armada de Las Dos Sicilias contra Trìpoli en 1828. Africa [Rome] Anno LVII, n.1, Marzo 2003, pp. 67-88. 6 JOHN WRIGHT particular) and their new-found humanitarian concerns, such as African slave trade abolition and the enslavement of Christians in Barbary. The 1828 episode also revealed a growing and dangerous diplomatic rivalry between Britain and France in Tripoli, which contributed in turn to the final crisis of the Karamanli regime, after more than a century of independent rule, and the re-establishment of direct Otttoman authority from Constantinople in 1835. The inept naval action, besides highlighting the defects of some contemporary, misused weaponry, also suggested that the Kingdom of the Two Sicilies had little hope of fulfilling ambitions of becoming a leading European power; in 1861 it was to be absorbed into the new Kingdom of Italy. Historical Background Piracy had been endemic in the Mediterranean since the late Roman Empire lost its command of the sea. In the Middle Ages, assaults by both Christian and Muslim freebooting outlaws were not necessarily confined to ships or coastal settlements of the opposing faith. From the 12th to the 15th centuries, relations between both sides of the Mediterranean were characterised equally by trade and piracy. The early 16th century became the “heroic age” of pirates who played a leading role in the all-out Mediterranean war between Spanish-led...",
