      {
        "identifier": "https://openalex.org/W2061241943",
        "title": "The Myth of Alpheus and Arethusa and Open-Sea Voyages on the Mediterranean-Stellar Navigation in Antiquity",
        "creator": [
          "Tomislav Bilić"
        ],
        "archival_reference": null,
        "specialist_agent": "contradiction",
        "description": "The article offers a new interpretation of the Greek myth of Alpheus and Arethusa, which it is believed emerged as a description of latitude-sailing between the Peloponnese and Sicily during the Bronze Age. The author discusses the stars which could have assisted navigation on this route. This is followed by a discussion on the ancient practice of determining latitude by observing the altitude of the lower culmination of the last circumpolar star. Some sea-routes are suggested which could have been traversed using the latitude-sailing technique, and the stars that could have been used as navigation aids on such voyages. © 2008 The Author",
