      {
        "identifier": "10.1093/oso/9780192857378.003.0007",
        "title": "Raising Ransoms",
        "creator": [
          "Bernard Capp"
        ],
        "archival_reference": null,
        "specialist_agent": "contradiction",
        "description": "Abstract Chapter 6 provides the first in-depth exploration of how ransom funds were raised in England, and the roles played by families and friends, merchants, local magistrates, the Church, and the state. It begins by showing how families learned of the fate of their loved ones, and the nature of contacts between Barbary and Britain. Captives of higher status often had the contacts and resources to negotiate ransoms privately. The poor, by contrast, depended on help from others. But who was responsible for providing it, and how? The chapter explores how wives, armed with a ‘brief’, were permitted to roam the country begging alms towards a ransom, or for their own relief. The state refused to pay ransoms to those it saw as pirates. But it became involved at arm’s length, issuing nationwide ‘general briefs’ which provided for charitable collections in every parish church. This system, introduced in 1624, was replaced in the civil war era by a surcharge on customs, the ‘Algiers duty’, but was revived after the Restoration. The chapter ends by assessing the strengths and weaknesses of both arrangements, and compares their record against the performance of the systems developed in continental Europe.",
        "subject": null,
        "context_excerpt": null,
        "mediatype": null,
