s in non-fireproof shops. in other shops, steel concrete was used for columns, and girders. Later (1934 and on) it became more widely used. The soviet msthads of planning and their technical standards are close to the 0erean standards and differ sharply from US methods. In Planning, the p!lmmi$1 attention was devoted to the economy of materials regardless of the increase in labor involved. 15. Only plain or smooth \"reinforcing bars\" were used. Thera were no \"deformed bars\". Ili connection. with this, all of the reinforcing bars had hooks bent at seek W. As a rule every building trust had a \"building yard\" Nhere the reinforcing bars were usually bent. The minimum size of the bars in use was 6 mm (1/4\"). Ia to RS the-mint\" sin of bars is 3/8\". Permissible stress in the UBB was 1200 kilograms per square centimeter 000 pounds per square inoh. The xinimun percentage of reinforcement is hhhhhh !!! of that in the US. Shear stress is bores mainly by bent bars and stirrups. STTm&wr Si'.QL -KC AS `.:. c ue, a part of shear stress is borne by concrete) Stimps were Oligato ry along the satire lanwah of a girder. This ~ a LW safety factor for shearin i' bta d in the wp, bO it requires more labor ='MU'CH BSCUld'Y n Foomm STl RItJp Sanitized Copy Approved for Release 2011/06/30: CIA-RDP80-00809A000600040341-9 Sanitized Copy Approved for Release 2011/06/30: CIA-RDP80-00809A000600040341-9 CONFIDENTIAL/S,EQURITL flFORMtPION 16. Cement was of medium quality but very variable. Therefore, the use of concrete of high durability was difficult to achieve. Usually, concrete of ultimate compression stress of 110 kilograms per square centimeter (1600 pounds per sq-,rare inch) on the 28th day; was used, Concrete of 180 $il.ograms per square centimeter compression stress was used loss frequently. Concrete of 210 kilograms per square centimeter (3000 pounds per square inch) compression stress, which is in general use in the US, was used only on rare occasions in the USSR. An example was, the foundation of the \"Palace of Soviets\" (A spedial concrete plant was built for that purpose). The ' amount of cement used for bard_concretewas 250 kilograms per cubic meter. Since the U88R bad no ready mined. concrete, aggregates were usually delivered to the construction site in the form of separate unmixed sand and separate gravel. It was difficult to obtain a good granul:ometric (relationship of sand, gravel, cement and water) composition of aggregates. 17. The quality of concrete suffered considwbl?ly from insufficient mixing time. If the number of nsixinga exceeded 120 in ~dghtibqura,'e~t~lom.msy{tedstaomisses. Conoiete of disastrously poor quality was obtained by \"Btakhanovite wstheds\", when the number of mixings reach 500 per byght,hgurej ib,oonaretaasasusped out of the mixer immediately after the materials had been poured into it. Vibrators were seldom used. 18. Determination of stress and momenta in rigid frame constructions of reinforced concrete was done very carefully by exact methods. The use of methods of approximation so common in the Us was not reoomernded. Before World War II new standards of planning were issued, which were based on the estimate of ultimate stresses. 19. During th? first Five-Year Plan \"sectional Ferro-concrete\" (reinforced concrete) vas widely used, 1 e concrete coltmna g rd rs at \"construction rds\" and sae led' at the eonstand raostion site, (like w vance ve L E-I-BAR, 50X1-HUM CoNCaiTf $TI RRUPs The manufacture of readyiaade concrete products, however, did not r a smoothly, and gradually this type of construction was used less and less frequently, except for light slabs. ROD CosceiTg Vtsta.vp 20. Wooden truoturea - During-U& tint Five-year P111n, wooden structures were ?'a'J' UM In use. They were am used for shape woe daapr of fire existed (such as boiler houses, ifse.U forging shops, etc);. During the second Five-year Plan the grsatse`pirt of the 910 at .as were burnt down, rotted or caved in and wepo rtj1agid by n