      {
        "identifier": "https://openalex.org/W4416321422",
        "title": "Archipelagos of Resistance",
        "creator": [],
        "archival_reference": null,
        "specialist_agent": "contradiction",
        "description": "The colonization of the Americas begins in the Caribbean, but something about its landscape exceeds what can be captured. Édouard Glissant describes with an invented term, irrué, this excess; an openness and irruption of the real and the unreal. The coloniality of space both orders and nihilates. In its nihilating dimension, spaces are empty, erasing the knowledge and ways of life of non-European subjects, but make possible construction of a new order. The Caribbean archipelago offers an island bridge to resistant landscapes and resistant modes of belonging across the Americas. The Cuban writer Antonio Benítez-Rojo describes its “unstable condensations, turbulences, whirlpools” and its “uncertain voyages of signification,” itineraries that escape the map of order and exceed the legacy of destruction. What kind of knowledge, resistance, grows here not captured by order? Beyond or against the striations of modern order, does knowledge production and resistance flourish on this irrué ground?",
        "subject": null,
        "context_excerpt": null,
        "mediatype": null,
        "metadata_text": null,
        "snippet": null,
        "description_provenance": "OPENALEX_ABSTRACT_METADATA_NOT_INSPECTED_FULLTEXT",
