      {
        "identifier": "cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp08c01297r000300180003-9",
        "title": "CIA Reading Room cia-rdp08c01297r000300180003-9: THE PARACEL'S/SPRATLYS DISPUTE AND ITS POTENTIAL FOR RENEWED SINO-VIETNAMESE ARMED CONFLICT",
        "creator": "CIA Reading Room",
        "archival_reference": null,
        "specialist_agent": null,
        "description": "Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08C01297R000300180003-9 rr4 144S Asian 750 August 1982 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 The Paftcets./Sp'atl'ys Dispute and Its Potential for Renewed Sind-Vietnamese Armed Conflict: Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08C01297R000300180003-9 TABLE OF CONTENTS Pam INTRODUCTION . . . . . . . . . . . . PART I. A GEOGRAPHICAL OVERVIEW . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . The Paracel Archipelago The Spratly Archipelago PART II. A HISTORICAL REVIEW OF THE CONFLICTING CLAIMS . . . 17 Chinese Claims to the Paracels Vietnamese Claims to the Paracels Chinese Claim to the Spratlys Vietnamese Claims to the Spratlys Philippine Claim to the Spratlys The Malaysian Claim to the Spratlys Discussion PART III. THE ISLANDS' IMPORTANCE: STRATEGIC OR ECONOMIC . . . , ?40 Stretegic Consideration China and Offshore Oil Vietnam and Offshore Oil PART IV. THE MILITARY CAPABILITIES OF THE CLAIMANTS . . . . . . . 53 Naval Power China Air Forces China Vietnam The Soviet Military Presence PART V. THE POTENTIAL FOR RENEWED SINO-VIETNAMESE ARMED CONFLICT . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 67 Recent Incidents in the South China Sea Possible Ways to Resolve Some Conflicting Claims Some Future Courses of Action for China A possible Sino-Vietnam Conflict Conclusion Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08C01297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 INTRODUCTION On 19-20 January, 1974, a sharp armed clash took place between naval, air, and ground forces of the People's Republic of China (PRC) and the Republic of Viet-Nam (RVN) in the XiSha (Paracel) Islands, located approximately 350 kilometers southeast of Hainan Island, in the South China Sea. (Fig. 1)1 According to the PRC, this battle was precipitated by RVN naval units' harrassment of Chinese fishing vessels in the Paracels on January 15-19, which resulted in several Chinese fishermen killed or wounded.2 In response, the PRC sent at least 1.1 naval units to the area, including 2 Yulin-based destroyers armed with the 22-mile range SS-N-2 \"STYX\" surface-to-surface naval missile. The RVN naval force consisted of an unknown number of destroyer escorts, coast guard cutters, and patrol boats.3 During the naval action on January 19, the RVN claimed to have sunk one Chinese ship and badly damaged a second. The RVN apparently. lost a frigate, reportedly sunk by a \"STYX\" missile, while three other ships, including two destroyers, were damaged either by missiles or missile fragments.4 Meanwhile, Chinese troops landed.on Duncan Island (Dao gan qun dao) (Fig. 2)' and forced the small RVN regional forces contingent to withdraw to Robert Island (Gan quan dao). A half-company of RVN sailors was divided between Money island (Jin-yin-dao) and Pattle Island (Shan-hu dao). On the following day, January 20, a Chinese force (possibly of battalion strength), supported by MIG-21 fighter-bombers, overran the Vietnamese Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 1100 PECPLL'S 9?_FUCLIC OF Cii1;1,1' / -' \"laccler=~cld 1k,1 '+ah-p,n !lock (Suzon i::rn c rratae T~ Chancel :3eed Sark, {Chun -yeh) Thl-tu I. Itu Aba 1. 1 ) Prince Consort dank Vanrtiuard ?pink ---4 4 Y 'ta ?rlendshlp ~hoala~''` Vorthc:?u Luconin.\",Yfoai SD+ah LuF ?1.3 ~r .Ia ^.C1 lfca is ._`.)~ Scale 1: ,,Cr.0, CO 0 da. Leo : ~o )zo ariilc^.ater) .1. Islands in the South China Sea '.Stor;:&gt;- F30' 3 m; p) ?,:(Sp: atly ) r? 'r:;gglA kl loxeteraO, uZi3i m11^_) Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 ,; Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Kuang-rein I. ! Ch err -har.9 j. Duncan 1. Fig.2. The Region Where Fighting Took Place on January 19.and 20, L974: The Crescent Group (Paracei Islands) Scale: 1 cm = approx. 2 km Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 positions on all three islands,6 capturing 48 RVN military personnel and one United States-civilian. 7 five .\"sick and wounded\" South Vietnamese were released to the International Red Cross in Hong Kong within one week,8 and the remaining 43 South Vietnamese and 1 American were released to I.R.C. officials on February 17. This two-day armed clash could hardly be described as- a \"major conflict,\" but the Chinese action was significant in three respects. .First, the PRC demonstrated its ability to project its naval power .beyond Chinese shores, as well as the ability to conduct joint air- sea-ground offensive operations.10 Second, the PRC demonstrated that it was both willing and able to back up territorial claims with armed force, lest anyone forget the lesson of the China-India border war of 1962. And third, the Chinese action in the Paracels served as a warning to Southeast Asian nations contesting Chinese claims to the Nansha (Spratly) Archipelago. As a result, attention was suddenly focused on the subject of the controversy, the islands themselves. How strong are the claims of the various claimants? What value do these islands have which could warrant the use of armed force to take and maintain them? Could tension among the claimants erupt into regional conflict, and if so, how would such a conflict affect regional stability? In the following paper, I will first present a geographical overview of the Paracel archipelagoes, followed by a historical review of the claims by the principal claimants. I will then attempt to explain the newly awakened interest in these islands which led to the 1.974 hostilities. After examining the strategic and economic significance of the islands, I will briefly review the military capabilities of the MMW-TeMlassified and A p ved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 .,~, Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 principal claimants and assess the ability of each to use such forces in support of their respective claims. Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 1. Chia, hung-dah, Chinese Attitude toward Contiliental Shelf and Its Implication on Delimiting Seabed in Southeast Asia, (B ltimore, University of Maryland, 1977 ,overleaf. 2. Morrow, Michael, \"Today Hsisha, Tomorrow .?\", Far Eastern Economic Review, Vol. 83, No. 4 (January 28, 1974), p. 32.' 3. Sardesai, .D.R., \"Vietnam's Quest for Security,\" in Changing Patterns of Security and Stabilityl,in Asia,(New York, Praeger: 1980),p, 233. 4. News Review on China, Mongolia, and the Koreas (hereafter called News ReviewT, New Delhi: Institute for Defense Studies and Analyses), February, 1974), pp. 121-123. 5. Heinzig, Dieter, Disputed Islands in the South China Sea, (Wiesbaden: Institute of Asian Affairs, Hamburg, 1976), p. 51. 6. News Review, (January, 1974), pp. 70-71. 7. News Review, (January, 1974), p. 183. 8. Vietnam Report, (15 February, 1974), p. 4. 9. News Review, (March 1974), p. 183. 10. Pollack, Jonathan D., \"China as a Military Power,\" in Military Power and Policy in Asian States: China, India, Japan,\\Bou2.Jer, Colo.:. Westview Pr?ess,1980, pp. 67-68. Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Part I: A Geographical Overview The semi-enclosed South China Sea covers about 3,685,000 square kilometers and is of the utmost strategic and economic importance. On the southwest, it communicates with the Indian Ocean through the vital Malacca-Singapore Straits and on the northeast with the Taiwan Strait and the East China Sea. It represents a major portion of the navigation route between the Pacific and Indian Ocean for the United States and Soviet navies as well as a vital trade link, especially for Japanese merchant marine vessels and oil tankers.' Scattered across the South China Sea are more than 170 islets, =shoals, coral reefs, banks, sands, cays, and rocks which are grouped in four mid-ocean coral archipelagoes: the Pratas Reef, the Macclesfield Bank, the Paracel Islands, and the Spratly Islands (Fig. 3.).2 The ownership of the Pratas Reef and the Macclesfield Bank, both situated- .closer to China than to either Vietnam or the Philippines, has not been contested; these are Chinese.3 However, ownership of the two larger archipelogoes, the Paracels and Spratlys, has been contested by China and Vietnam for some 180 years, and in recent years three other govern- ments, the Republic of China,.the Republic of the Philippines, and Malaysia, have declared their claims to one or more of the Spratlys. The Paracel Archipelago The Paracel archipelago comprises 15 islets and more than one-dozen partly or temporarily submerged reefs and banks, scattered over an area forming a large oval with a maximum diameter of about 200 km. Its center Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Yu ON 1~HO CHI NINNH CITY PARACEL S i.~ PRATAS N'ACCLESF ELD [.LANK 2O'- PHfL1P ' HNES SPRATL Y. V 500 KM /'.; Archipelagoes in the South China Sea TAIYVAN Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 is approximately midway between the southern coast of Hainan Island (about 350 km southeast of Yulin) and the central Vietnam coast (about 400 km east of Da Nang). 4 The Paracels are generally subdivided into two principal groups, the Amphtrite Group, located in the northeastern part of the archipelago and comprising seven islands, eight cays, and one reef (Fig. 4)5, and the Crescent Group which is located in the southwestern part of the archipelago and comprises eight islands, four partly submerged reefs, and a and cay (Fig. 5).6 Since the'Paracels are claimed by both the PRC and the SRV, most of the islands have three names. The chart in Fig. 6 lists each by its English, Chinese, and Vietnamese names as well as its size (where known).9 The Spratly Archipelago The Spratly.Islands are an archipelago in the South China Sea composed of more than 100 islands, shoals, reefs, banks, and cays, stretching over an area of 180,000 sq. km.7 The center of the group is located approximately 1,450 km. southwest of Taiwan, 1,000 km. south of Hainan Island, 700 km.. south of the Paracels, 500 km. southeast of Vietnam, and 400 kni. west of Palawan Island in the Philippine-s, Me principal regions in the Spratlys, with their islands, reefs and cays are detailed in Fig. 710 and shown on map in Fig. 8.11 All of the islands are located in the tropical zone. The average annual temperature in this region varies between 15 and 28? C. Annual rainfall averages 2,500 mm. The islands' flora consists primarily of pineapple, coconut palms, tung oil, , quince, and banana trees, The islands have been, and still are, economically important as bases for fishing vessels and as sites of valuable guano deposits, Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 xidAl~l. ~.`~~p3curh I. ~ `~7~5outnSxnd Arnphitri t croup r3a :,i.ca/n I. i?~ NlP~Y/,I / nkf ~' Fig.4. The Amphitrite Group (Paracel Islands) 10 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 rig.5. The Crescent Group (paracel Islands) Scale: 1 cm = approx. 2) kmm POW07meclassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08C01297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved F