 61. Ronquillo, Bernardino, \"Manila's Quest for Securit ,\" Far Eastern Economic Review, Vol. 83, No. 8 (February 25, 1974), p. 28. 62. Chang, Pao-Min, \"The Sino-Viethamese Territorial Dispute,\" Asia Pacific No. 8, (Spring 1980), p. 134. ...; 63. \"The Truth about the Sino-Vietnamese Boundary ues ion, a jing Review, Vol. 22, No. 21 (25 May 1979), pp. 24-25. 64. , \"Hanoi Goes Back on Its Word,\" Beijing*Review, Vol. 22, No. 3.4, (August 24, 1979), p. 24. 'i^a^tie Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 _ ~r_. Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 65. Chang, p. 136. 66. Ibid 67. \"Quarterly Chronicle and Documentation,\" China Quarterly, No. 58, (April-June 1974), p. 431. 68.', Chang, pp. 137-138. 1p69. Chang, p. 140. ,, t !' 1 x30. Chang, pp. 144-145. 71.. Chang. p. 153. 1'? Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 39 L?..... t i i -1. - Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 40 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 ?~, 4 ~~ .?: Part III: The Importance of the Islands: Strategic or Economic? In assessing the importance of the Paracel and Spratly archipelagoes to China and Vietnam, all observers immediately identify two important factors: their strategic geographical position in the South China Sea, and the potential presence of large deposits of oil. I would suggest that while China sees the islands in terms of both these considerations, Vietnam's primary interest is in the latter. Strategic Consideration A quick glance at a map of Southeast Asia confirms that the Paracel and Spratly archipelagoes sit astride the main sea lanes through the South China Sea.I These lanes are used by international shipping moving from the oil fields-of the Middle East, through the Indian Ocean and the Malacca Strait enroute to East Asia. This is Japan's oil life- line, and it is estimated that every 10 minutes, a tanker bearing crude oil from the Persian Gulf transits the Malacca Strait enroute to Japanese refindries.2 The Japanese recognized the strategic importance of these islands when during World War II they used the Paracels as an advanced submarine base.3 Even today, anchorages, supply depots, defensive monitoring and intelligence-gathering stations could be established in either or both archipelagoes, to provide a critical strategic advantage.4 China has already so fortified the Paracels.5 These same sea lanes are also the main line of movement for Soviet submarines and surface ships of the Soviet Far East fleet between?the Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Indian Ocean and Far East Fleet Headquarters at Vladivostok. Soviet naval units also use these waters enroute to and from the newly- acquired naval and air bases at Da Nang and Cam Ranh Bay in Vietnam. Under.existing international law, the coastal state which occupies the islands in an archipelago could conceivably apply the archipelagic principle and claim, in addition to the immediately adjacent waters, ;a 200-mile economic. resources zone around the entire archipelago, determined from the outermost point of each island. Any state in control of the Paracels and Spratlys could place a substantial part of the South China Sea under its jurisdiction. The coastal state would have sovereign ,rights within the zone for the purposes of exploiting, conserving, and managing the resources of the seabed, subsoil, and the seas above,6 All the claimants in this case, i.e., China, Vietnam, Taiwan, the Philippines, and Malaysia, realize that when technologically poor nations cannot exploit ocean resources, the only benefit is to stake a claim7 and manage the exploitation. It is expected that within this economic zone, a large proportion .of the traditional high seas will remain; freedom of navigation and overflight will be preserved.8 However. there are niceties which could be applied to impede or even prohibit certain naval traffic, For f;? example, should the PRC control the Spratlys, it could cite environ- i mental safeguards to prohibit Soviet nuclear-powered vessels from C transiting the waters. It could also invoke that portion of the doctrine ,~..of innocent paStage which gives a coastal or archipelagic state the tight to \"suspend temporarily the innocent passage of foreign ships if such suspension is essential for the protection of its security.\"' 41 L i_``. , ;~ it f .L, G .. Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 \"Acts prejudicial to security\" would include: 1) threat or use of force, 2) exercises with weapons, 3) launching or taking aboard air- craft, and 4) research or survey activities of any kind.9 In the summer of 1979, shortly after signing agreements with four U.S. oil companies for joint prospecting for offshore oil in the Gulf of Tonkin., China reiterated its claim to the Paracels and then announced four \"danger areas'.! adjacent to Hainan Island and the Paracels, closing them to both naval and air activity, the latter to include all altitudes between 1,000 and 20,000 meters. While the Chinese gave no reason for this action, there was speculation that it was a bid to achieve international acceptance of territorial claims.10 Thus, possession-of the two archipelagoes would give China the potential to control movement in the South China Sea, and enable China to break the Soviet Union's military encirclement of China. China might conceivably even be able to force the Soviet fleet to go around Indonesia and the Philippines in order to reach Vladivostok.11,Po.ss'ess.i.on of the Spratlys by the SRV, on the other hand, would indirectly provide those same economic and strategic benefits to the Soviet Union to be used against Japan, China, Korea, or the United States. China and Offshore Oil Although the average depth of the South China Sea is about 1,600 meters, the northern portion (the China.Basin) averages about 4,300 meters and the Palawan Trough averages from nearly 3,000 meters to a iiidi1mUiri depth of over 5,000 meters off Palawan Island 12 IL Both the Paracel End Spratly Islands are situated in the deep basin area,13 with average depths of almost 1,000 meters around the Paracels Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 . Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 on drilling technology, even that inherited from the Russian presence 1i in the 1950s, is especially rudimentary. This is attested by the con- b J e s capa ilIty to exploit. In addition to greath depths, environmental factors (i.e., wave frequency .and height, bottom conditions,-surface currents, and wind/typhoon exposure) greatly complicate drilling operations. The PRC's offshore and about 3,000 meters around the Spratlys.14 Generally speaking, those portions of the South China Sea which lie beyond China's continental ' \" -I5a shelf are for the present he~ond th PRC' tinuing imports of.oil-drilling, extraction, and transportation equipment from Japan, Romania, the USSR, and the United States.16 However, since the late 1950s, the PRC has shown increasing interest in the South ChinaiSea and its potential bonanza of offshore oil. Beginning in 1968, scientists from the Institute of Oceanography on the South China Sea began investigations around the islands claimed by the PRC. According to the Chinese press, \"these investigations included marine gravity, magnetism, hydrography, meterology, biology, chemistry, and geology with the aim of helping exploit the resources in the South } China Sea and develop marine science.\" 17 .- However, although the work was ultimately postponed, it was the DRV which took the initiative in exploring the area when it informed the PRC in late 1973 of its intention to prospect for oil in the Gulf of Tonkin. In 1977, the PRC announced that a \"scientific investigation ship\" .. had t veyed areas between the Paracels and Spratlys,during a 58-day, 4;926 nautical mile voyage for the Institute of Oceanography on the South China Sea under the Chinese Academy of Sciences. The same news Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 release announced that five other voyages had been made to the Paracels and Spratlys \"within the last ten years.\"20 In November 1978, at least three oil rigs were in place west\"\" of Hainan Island and light crude oil was found there.21 During the early 1970s, China's oil exploration activity in the \"South China Sea was constrained not only by the technical difficulties ,and cost involved, but,also by political uncertainties. China's oil planners are now'aware that many of the promising areas in the South China Sea are now, including areas near the Paracel and Spratly archi pelagoes, within the reach of drilling technology, or will be by the late 1980s.22 However, many of these areas appear likely to remain b A 2 eyon estern production techniques until perhaps the 1990s. 3 There- fore, an interim goal is to acquire Western technology, gain experience in all phases of managing joint-ventures in oil exploration and production, and most importantly, secure recognition of China's territorial claims by the time technological advances make possible.the full exploitation of China's oil resources. Beijing's stated plan is to double oil production.by the year 2000. ,This will be done almost entirely by massive exploitation of China's offshore oil reserves, estimated in 1981 to be nearly 74 billion barrels, ,'which could make China the world's fourth largest producer of oil.24 By 1986, exploration costs in the South China Sea alone are expected to reach US $250-300 million per year. Plans call for the drilling ,, of 110 Wildcat exploratory wells and 100 evaluation and confirmation wd1ls in areas of the Pearl River Estuary, the Yinggehai Basin, and the Gulf of Tonkin.25 44 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Given all these expectations it is unlikely that China will relinquish claims to territories which possibly contain-oil deposits, and highly probable that the PRC will take any action necessary, including the military option, to protect those claims. Vietnam af1d'Offshore Oil During the French colonial period in Vietnam, an active interest was sustained in searching for oil throughout Indochina,.especially ' after the Dutch discoveries in Indonesia. However, nothing came of . these attempts, which ceased in 1945 with the collapse of French authority. 26 In 1967, using U.S. Navy oceanographic ships, the United Nations ;?Economic Commission for Asia and the Far East (ECAFE) mapped the region extending from the Sea of Japan south along the coast of China and around the Indochinese peninsula into the Gulf of Siam. While the initial findings were discouraging, the geologists did find a desirable drilling area on the continental shelf, southeast of South .Vietnam containing a projected 140,000 cubic statute miles of oil sediments'. A larger area is said to extend from southwest of the F$ tISpratly Islands, looping around toward the Sarawak coast, south of t',the Spratlys',27 `Magnetic field survey data fromProject MAGNET of the U.S, Naval Oceanographic Office, was analyzed by the German Geological Survey for a geophysical interpretation, and two large sediment-filled basins were Identified; namely, the Can Tho and Ca Mau basihs of the Mekong Delia'. 28 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 According to Dr. K. O. Emery, a marine biologist of the Woods .Hole Oceanographic Institute and an advisor to ECAFE, the Asian coastal area is equal in potential to the Middle East or to the oil fields of Venzuela and the Gulf of Mexico combined,29 more significantly, the basin off South Vietnam is particularly desirable because local oil sediments there are'low in sulphur.30 During 1969 and 1970, with the permission of the RVN government, a detailed survey of the Vietnamese southeastern continental shelf was carried out by a consortium of private oil companies. The results were sufficiently-encouraging by mid-1973 for the RVN to solicit bids. Thirty-eight companies from six nations responded and four U.S. oil companies and a Candian group were granted exploration rights on Vietnam's continental shelf.31 The security situation in the Mekong Delta precluded any exploration/exploitation activity. The first well was begun in August 1974, and by March 1975, the exploration program had made significant progress and was continuing to gain momentum. However, in April, because of the deterioration of the military situation, all the oil rigs were towed away and the personnel were withdrawn.32 The extent of the deposits remains unknown. The former RVN had placed great hopes on the discovery of offshore oil as being one of the solutions to the country's economic woes. The oil could have provided a source of badly needed hard currency-if only to offset a portion of the nation's costly oil imports. Vietnam had few other mineral resources and agricultural development was either retarded or destroyed by the war. Additionally, after the American withdrawal in 197333 the RVN faced an ever-dwindling flow of U.S. economic and military support. -IMWXM Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 The early stages of the oil exploration program did indeed contribute significantly to the RVN economy in the form of taxes and fees levied against concessionaire companies.34 Unfortunately, the dream of oil riches might also have touched off a spark of nationalism which re-ignited regional territorial disputes between Vietnam and Cambodia, and Vietnam and.Indonesia, and might also have been the reason behind the RVN move on, the Paracels in January 1974. Finally, oil may have been an added element in convincing the North Vietnamese to seek a final victory on the battlefield, fearing that oil discoveries might become?a critical factor in determining the course of the Vietnam War, i.e.., a fresh source of economic support for the Thieu regime.35 In 1973, the DRV*was also taking steps toward exploration for =oil. According to Japanese sources, prospects for oil in North Vietnam are also bright. Two Japanese oil companies competed with French and Italian companies for bids on offshore exploration in the Gulf of Tonkin;36 however, so as not to annoy China, Hanoi reportedly later abandoned the idea,. Immediately after the conquest of South.Vietnam, Hanoi invalidated oil contracts signed with the RVN government, an action which cost U.S. oil companies, whom Hanoi had accused of \"plundering\" Vietnam's resources, some $50 million invested in exploration and a comparable figure in'distribution facilities.38 However, in August 1975, Hanoi created a cabinet-level national oil and gas agency and officially .:,.announced-its willingness to renegotiate the terms for offshore oil exploration with any foreign oil company ready to participate in the country's search for oil, Negotiations were initiated with Norwegian, Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Japanese, and Algerian companies for various forms of help in exploration.- The French began negotiations for a contract to construct a logistics base at the seaport of Vung Tau, and Norway agreed to provide an off- ; shore drilling rig and technical training in its use. The PRG even approached American companies and invited them to return; however, the t United States trade embargo against Vietnam brought that effort to a standstill.39 In 1976, Hanoi hired a French company to conduct seismic surveys in the offshore areas near south Vietnam which had previously been explored by the RVN.40 Since that time, seismic and exploration contracts have been awarded to Canadian, Italian, and West German concerns. Vietnam's entrance into COMECON, coupled with the U.S. trade embargo, have paved the way for an agreement with five COMECON partners, the Soviet Union,'Bulgaria, Czechoslovakia, Hungary, and Poland, to aid Vietnam in surveys for oil in projects running from 1981 to 1990.41 Judging from the speed with which the SRV has entered into the \"oil game,\".it would appear that, like the RVN, the SRV is also antici- pating the discovery of offshore oil in order to rescue its economy, r which eight years after the war, remains in disarray due in part to Vietnam's Cambodian war, the costs of rebuilding both North and South, and general mismanagement of the entire economy. According to United States State Department estimates, by, the end of the fiscal year .ending in June 1980, the Soviet Union had poured $1 billion of economic and military aid into Vietnam, and that aid/continues at a rate of Over $3 million per day.42 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 In July 1981, Vietnam and the Soviet Union signed an agreement to establish a joint enterprise to survey and explore for oil and gas on the continental shelf of South Vietnam. According to Do Muoi, Vice- Chairman of the Council of Ministers, \"thousands of Soviet cadres and ,.among observers as to how'much Moscow would be able to achieve in off- shore exploration, given its own technical weaknesses in this field, Hanoi appeared optimistic that with Soviet aid,. it could begin to exploit offshore oil reserves by 1983 .4 Given the PRC's renewed drive for oil exploration in the Gulf of Tonkin, (the delineation of which is still contested by the SRV), China's on-shore discoveries in the Paracels (which Vietnam also claims), and Vietnam'.sintention to explore for oil in offshore areas close to the Spratlys (claimed by China but several islands of which are SRV-occupied), it would appear that the quest for oil could = experts are now cooperating with us in oil-exploration and exploitation along the continental shelves in an effort to help our country quickly obtain oil and natural gas.\"43 Although there was considerable skepticism f eventually lead to renewed armed conflict between China and Vietnam. a: E Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 ?_,_ Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 NOTES Chiu, Hung-dah, \"South China Sea Islands: Implication for Delimiting the Seabed and Future Shipping Routes,\" China Quarterly, No. 72 .Black, Edwin F., \"Dangers Facing Thailand,\" Hono1ulu'Star-Bulletin, (N e b 1 ov m er 3, 981), p. A-14. Ghosh, S. K., \"Rivairy'in the South China Sea \" China Report Vol 13 , , . , No. 2-(March-April 1977), p. 6. - Feeney, William R., \"Dispute Settlement, the Emerging Law of the Sea, and East Asian Maritime Boundary Conflicts,\"'Asian'Profile, Vol. .8,-No. 6 (December 1980), p. 585. 5. Qang, Zhong-pu, \"Visit to the Xisha Islands,\" China Reconstructs, Vol. 29, No. 9 (September 1980), pp. 22-28. Lee, Yong Leng, \"Offshore Boundary Disputes in Southeast Asia,\" Journal of Southeast Asian Studies, Vol. 10, No. 1 (March 1979), p. 178. \";- -Dangerous Group for Asian Peace \" 'Asian' 'Sure, Vol 17 N 12 Copper, John F. \"China's Claim to South China Sea Islands,\" China 'Report, Vol. 10, No. 3 (May-June 1974), p. 11. Lee, p. 178. ; ;9. Katchen, Martin H., \"The Spratly Islands and the law' of 1-he Sea (December 1977), p. 1174-5. . '?+ 10. Chanda, Nayan, \"Same Show, New Theatre,\" Far Eastern Economic'Review,. 4,1 '-,Vol. .105, No. 38 (September 21, 1979), pp. 13-14. r 11. Copper, p. 13. ``'' j 12. ''Chiu, p. 758. ,13. Feeney, p. 584. 14. Chiu, p. 756. 15. Hardy, Randall W,, China's Oil Future: A'Case of Modest ' Expectations, (Bou der, Co.: Westview Press), 1978, p. 21. 16. Smi1, Vaclov, China's Energy, (New York: Praeger Publishers, 1976), p. 180, ' 50 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 \"South China Sea Investigated,\" Beijing Review, Vol. 21, No. 5 (February 3, 1978), p. 30. 18. Harrison, Selig S., China, Oil, and Asia: Conflict Ahead? (New York: Columbia University Press, 1977 , p. 283, n. 59. 19. Feeney, p. 586. . 20. , \"South China Sea Investigated,\"' Peking' Review, Vol. 21, No. 5 (February 3, 1978), p. 30. 21. Parker, Guy J., \"The Security Implications of Regional Energy and Natural Resource Exploitation\" in Asian Security in'the 1980s, Richard H. Solomon, ed., (Santa Monica, CA: Rand Corporation, 1979), p. 243. - 22. Harrison, p. 54. 23. Harrison, pp. 193-194. 24. \"China Sea Holds Vast Oil Reserves,\" Honolulu Star- Bulletin, April 5, 1982), p. A-11. 25. Rivers, Clarence, \"China's 10-Billion-Tonne Offshore Oil Bonanza,\" Far Eastern Economic Review, Vol. 114, No. 41 (October 2, 1981), pp. 57-58. 26. Brown, David G., \"Vietnam's Petroleum Resources,\" Asian Survey, Vol. 16, No. 6 (June 1976), pp. 553-554. 27. \"A Special Correspondent,\" \"Of Oil and Island,\"'Vietnarh Report, Vol. 3, (February 15, 1974), p. 13. -.28. Brown, p. 558. 29. Adie, Ian W.A.C., \"China's Oil Boomerang,\" Asia Quarterly, 1976, No. 2, p. 105. 30. 'Special Correspondent, p. 13. 31. Brown, p. 559. 32. Brown, p. 560. 33. Brown, p.. 562, 34. 'Ibid. 35. Harrison, Selig S., China Oil and Asia: Conflict Ahead? (New York: Columbia University Press, 197 j, p, 190, 51 ,r' c a b,i i Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 4 .1 36. Cao, A.D., \"Vietnam: Investment and Trade Opportunities,\" Asia uarterl , 1977, No. 2, pp. 123. 37. Chanda, Nayan \"Sam Show, New Theatre,\" Far Eastern Economic Review, Vol. 105, No. 38 (September 21, 1979), p. 13. 38. Brown, pp. 567-568. 39. Woodard, Kim, The International Ener Relations of China, (Stanford, CA: Stanford University Press, 1980), p. 164. 40. Ng Shui Meng, t'Vietnam:.-A Year of Peace,\" Southeast Asian Affairs, (1976), p. 4831.1.. 41. Lacriat, George and Liu, Melinda, \"Pouring Trouble on Oily Waters,\" Far Eastern Economic Review, Vol. 105, No. 39 (September 20, 1979), p. 20. 42. Black, Edwin'F., \"Dangers Facing Thailand,\" Honolulu Star-Bulletin, .(November 3, 1981), p. A-14. 43. Long, Ton, \"An Embrace for the Bear,\" Far Eastern Economic Review, Vol. 113, No. 35,-(August 21, 1981), p. 48. 44. Gray, Denis D., \"Vietnam's Economy Improves,\" Honolulu Star-Bulletin, (March 16, 1982), p. A-15. - Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 ' : a:i Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Part IV: The Military Capabilities of the Claimants During the 1974 clash in the Paracels, for several reasons, the PRC had a decided advantage over the RVN forces. First, the Chinese \"reaction\" was a carefully-planned and coordinated, well-executed combined arms operation, involving aircraft, ships, and infantry. The Chinese People's Liberation Army (PLA) had employed such tactics against Hainan and Quemoy in 1949-50, Yikiangshan and the Tachens in 1954-55, and Quemoy again in 1958. The RVN forces had n.o such experience. Reportedly, the PLA used its Canton Military Region Headquarters as the command post for the operation, and therefore had a trained battle- operations staff and well-established command and control communications throughout the course of the battle. The PLA also had the advantages of nearby airbases on Hainan island and radar on nearby Yung-Xing (Woody) Island,3 while almost all the RVN's F-5 fighters were based at Tan Son Nhut airfield, near Saigon. Further, no Vietnam Air Force (VNAF) fighter pilot had ever seen aerial combat and had little or'no experience working with the controllers at the Tactical Air Control Center North Sector (TACC/NS) outside of Danang, who would have had directed the fighters. None of the controllers had ever performed an actual ground-controlled-intercept (GCI). On the sea, the Chinese had a similar advantage. The RVN coast- guard cutters, destroyer escorts, and patrol boats were clearly no match for the Chinese task force which included at least four destroyers and a number of short-range missile-firing, fast attack craft (FAC's).4 Militarily and psychologically, RVN military commanders were more Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 concerned with holding off the Vietnamese Communist threat than they were a Chinese attack from which the Vietnamese had emerged with only light losses and only the loss of island territory, the defense of which was not militarily feasible. And finally, the northern half of Vietnam was then the Democratic Republic of Vietnam, China's ally, and was hardly in a position to speak out against an attack on its enemy, the Thie-u regime, let alone come to their defense. One year later, the situation had -changed drastically. The RVN had been toppled by the DRV in a lightning-swift final offensive, and the Vietnamese people were. now, theoretically, unified and by 1979 would revive their traditional anti-Chinese brand of nationalism to challenge Chinese claims to the Paracels and Spratly groups. Naval Strengths After 1974, the PRC increased its military presence in the South China Sea. In July 1976 and August 1977, it held joint air-sea-amphibious maneuvers along the southeast coast,5 and embarked on a program to strengthen the `navy overall. The South Sea Fleet, with some 600 vessels deployed from Dong Shan to the Vietnam frontier, received its due share. In May and June, 1980, an 18-ship task force made a 35-day, 8,000 nautical mile foray into the South Pacific, accompanied by new ocean-going supply ships, and one year later, a destroyer-led flotilla from the South Sea Fleet made an operational cruise into the South China Sea.6 On the surface, it would appear that China at last has a \"blue water\" navy, i.e., one capable,of a greater role than that of coastal defense. If China intends, as it obviously does, to continue to press claims to the islands in the South China Sea, especially the Spratlys, and to - Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 assert complete control over a 200-mile economic zone for its continental as well as island territories, then a long-term development program oriented toward a \"blue water\" navy is exactly what China requires.7 But how effectively can China utilize this naval force to enforce its territorial claims? Fig. 9 8 shows a comparison of the PRC and SRV navies in the types of naval craft required for such operations. As with most aspects of the Chinese military machine, numbers alone are not entirely indicative: Although one single unlocated nuclear attack submarine would be of great concern to any surface fleet, China would probably be loath to.risk detection of either of the two HAN-class nuclear submarines by_the Soviet TU-95F anti-submarine patrol aircraft operating out of Da Nang and Cam..Ranh Bay,9 and Chinese diesel patrol submarines have only a 7,500 mile maximum cruising range and can remain at sea only 35-45 days.10 The missile-equipped destroyers are armed with an obsolete missile, and have no effective surface-to-air-missile (SAM) system, a primitive anti-submarine warfare (ASW) capability, and no`over-the-horizon (0TH) radar.11 While the fast attack craft (FAC) were very effective in the 1974 Paracels action, these are strictly short-range boats. This is probably one reason, along with inadequate air cover, why the Chinese in 1974 did not mount a similar operation against RVN garrisons in the Spratlys.12 The Chinese mine warfare capability has not yet been tested but could prove to be of great value in either an offensive or defensive role, simply because the Vietnamese have virtually no capability for laying or sweeping naval mines. Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 s C Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 giy !: Comparison of PRC and SRV Navies T e Shi Nuclear attack submarine' Ballistic missile submarine Diesel attack submarine Missile-equipped destroyers Frigates Corvette/patrol escort r Fast attack craft (missile) Fast attack craft (torpedo) Fast attack craft (gun) Fast attack craft (patrol) Large patrol craft Mine warfare craft Ocean minesweepers Landing ship, tank (LST) Landing ship, mechanized (LSM) Landing craft (various types) C SRV 0 0 0 0 6 2 2 HAN-class 1 GOLF-class 92 ROMEO/WHISKEY/MIND 12 (w/CSS-N-2 SSM) 16 (8 w/CSS-N-2) 9 188 255 376 24 21 80 23 15 16 475 (w/CSS-N-2) Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Although the Chinese have a large number of landing craft, the vast majority could not operate on the open sea. China is build- ing a new Alligator-class amphibious landing ship, but at present the Chinese Navy would be hard-pressed to move more than one infan- try division with light tanks (12,700 men, 3T-62/63 light tanks, 32 T-59 medium tanks),:arid this'could only be done over an unopposed ,beach or pier.13 The absence of a large-scale amphibious capability indicates that there is little chance in the near .future for PRC military planners to attempt to mount a major invasion.14 .Finally, although some destroyers are equipped with SAMs for air defense, Chinese ships are generally vulnerable to air attack, relying primarily on anti-aircraft cannon and machine guns.15 Air Forces Fig. 1016 shows the principal types of bombers, fighters, transports, and helicopters in the PLAAF inventory, while Fig. 11 shows operational envelopes from Hainan island, using the combat radii for the major aircraft types. As can be easily seen, the PLAAF would have no problem putting bombers, fighter-bombers, or interceptors over the Paracels; however, only one fighter, the MIG-19 is capable of operating over the Spratlys. PLAAF aircraft have never operated at such a great distance from the .mainland, and even if these could be kept under positive control (i.e., given optimum weather and communications), the MIG-19 would be at the extreme end of its combat radius with little or'no fuel to engage enemy .aircraft. Similarly, the TU-4, TU-16, and IL-28 could all be used over-the .Paracels, but only the TU-4 and TU-16 could actually deliver bombs on targets in the Spratlys. The TU-4 is a copy of the World War II U.S. ? r r'4 11 -ti`, , ,, I . . 57 ? Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08C01297R000300180003-9 IC Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 Fig. 10: PRC Air Force (PLAAF) Aircraft Role Radius (NM) Payloads (lbs) TU-16/BADGER Intermed-range bomber 1,650 6,600 TU-4/BULL Intermed-range bomber 1,550 10,000 IL-28/BEAGLE Medium-range bomber 550 2,200 TU-2/BAT Medium-range bomber 570 3,300 { MIG-15/FAGOT-: Fighter-bomber 150 Cannon, 2-550# bombs MIG-17/FRESCO .Interceptor 280 -Cannon, 2-550# bombs MIG-19/FARMER Interceptor 530 Cannon,2-550/1 bombs, msl MIG-21/FISHBED . -Interceptor 360 Cannon,2-550I bombs, msl F-9/FANTAN Fighter-bomber 430 Unknown LI-2/CAB Short-range transport 550 27 troops IL-12/COACH Short-range transport 650 32 troops IL-14/CRATE Short-range transport 550 26 troops AN-12/CUB Medium-range transport 910 100 troops AN-24/COKE Short-range transport 650 38 troops AN-26/CURL Short-range transport 650 80 troops MI-4/HOUND Medium helicopter 130 14 troops MI-6/HOOK Heavy helicopter 165? 90 troops MI-8/HIP Medium helicopter 115 24 troops SA-32/SUPER FRELON Heavy assault helicopter 220 30 troops Declassified and Approved For Release 2012/10/17: CIA-RDP08CO1297R000300180003-9 MOMM Fie. 1 i - nnPrAtinnnI Fnva'rnna from u~,,,",
