      {
        "finding_id": "section-ec9f04d317a65395d3cd",
        "file": "893cb321e932ba3a878252fb.txt",
        "title": "Images of the Mediterranean in an Ottoman Pirate Novel from the Late Seventeenth Century",
        "url": "https://makale.isam.org.tr/server/api/core/bitstreams/e9b22c6b-e4de-482a-be2e-33b284a17014/content",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": null,
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "9348e8969051079250376bb40ba76a85a2807ca05ec788735fb51803f5573fd4",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Venetian ships are not mentioned at all in the text.32 The author speaks as if the\nsole opponent of the Ottoman-cum-Maghrebine navy was the Maltese corsairs.33\nThe ommission of the Venetian naval power can be explained again by the fact\nthat the author seems to be familiar only with the itinerary Alexandria-Istanbul,\nin a period after the fall of Candia when the Venetians were active mainly in the\nwestern Aegean and the Adriatic, all the more so since their trading activity had\nseverely declined;34 however, by no means was Venetian martial activity absent in\nthe area even in this period, which makes its omission in the text still puzzling.35\nWhat, then, is the mental image the author of the text has of the Mediterranean? He clearly does not have in mind a map the way we do, with its winds and\npoints of the compass. One could even say there is no Mediterranean Sea in the\nnarrative; there are only itineraries. Or, in other words and to extend the simile\nwith mapping, there is no mental portolan or mappamundi, but only a mental\nisolarium, a list of island descriptions.36 The very incoherence ",
