      {
        "finding_id": "section-488b1783eac97456538c",
        "file": "893cb321e932ba3a878252fb.txt",
        "title": "Images of the Mediterranean in an Ottoman Pirate Novel from the Late Seventeenth Century",
        "url": "https://makale.isam.org.tr/server/api/core/bitstreams/e9b22c6b-e4de-482a-be2e-33b284a17014/content",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": null,
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "9348e8969051079250376bb40ba76a85a2807ca05ec788735fb51803f5573fd4",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Abstract  “The discourse on Warden Captain Mahmud, on [his] victories over the\ndamned dwellers of Hell, the Maltese” is a rare specimen of Ottoman Tiefkultur novel of\nthe late seventeenth century. Allegedly copied from a manumitted slave’s letter to his former\nmaster, it describes the former’s adventures when he set off from Alexandria to Istanbul.\nWritten in a simple and lively language, this valuable text gives a great deal of information\non intra-religious relationships in the Mediterranean, on the life of corsairs, but also on the\nways Mediterranean seamen conceived the geography of the Sea, planned and understood\ntheir itineraries, viewed the various nations acting in the Levant, and so forth. After a study\nof the composition of the text and of its relations with similar texts in Ottoman literature,\nthis paper tries to answer questions such as what type of ‘cognitive/mental map’ would\nOttoman seamen have in mind in order to represent a known sea and its itineraries, what\ngeographical markers did they use, how did they perceive a given sea. Analysis takes into\naccount real maps, portolani and isolaria, as well as other similar sources.\nKeywords: Mediterranean, piracy, Ottoman literature, mental maps.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
