      {
        "finding_id": "section-cf5bcfe972c3dfb82913",
        "file": "bookofpirates0000gilb_bookofpirates0000gilb_djvu.txt",
        "title": "The book of pirates",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/bookofpirates0000gilb",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Gilbert, Henry, 1868-1937",
          "date": [
            "1916-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "1901bb10294e81f32d98c63cba8e03792d5ae5388c5c8aacfc5b904d67fab94d",
        "evidence_excerpt": "However insolent and arrogant the pirates had been, \nthey had not hitherto dared to interfere greatly with the \ncorn-ships which went to and fro between the Tiber and \nEgypt, carrying corn from the fertile lands of the Nile \nto fill the granaries of Rome. Nor did they cut off the \ntraders plying to the City of the Seven Hills. But about \nthe year 78 B.c. they had become so daring as to stop \nthe trade from Rome, and then the senators had to bestir \nthemselves. They determined to root out the pirates \nfrom their lairs in Cilicia, and for this purpose they sent \nthe pro-consul Servilius, at the head of a squadron, to \ndestroy them utterly. In three difficult campaigns in the \ncreeks and among the mountains of Cilicia he inflicted \ngreat losses upon the pirates, and on returning to Rome \nhe was given a triumph. But the buccaneers were only \nscotched, not killed.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
