      {
        "finding_id": "section-0fd71887e9ff865dfc48",
        "file": "bookofpirates0000gilb_bookofpirates0000gilb_djvu.txt",
        "title": "The book of pirates",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/bookofpirates0000gilb",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Gilbert, Henry, 1868-1937",
          "date": [
            "1916-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "1901bb10294e81f32d98c63cba8e03792d5ae5388c5c8aacfc5b904d67fab94d",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Some of the pirates—great hairy men, with cruel \nfaces—stood with axes in hand guarding the captives, \nwhile others were ranged about the boats as if to help \nin pushing them down. On the shore before the captives, \nwhose wan faces spoke of hunger, ill-treatment and terror, \nstood one old Saxon, of a great stature, lavishly adorned \nwith gold chains about his neck and upon his shirt of \nmail, while his huge arms were encircled by rings of the \nsame material. On his head he wore a steel cap, from \nbeneath which his white hair hung to his shoulders. \nWith this man were three others, and one of them \nseemed to be counting the prisoners. Elphin counted \nwith him, and saw that every tenth person was drawn out \nand thrust aside. Thus there was soon a little group of \nsome twenty persons, men, women, and children, who \nlooked at each other and at their captors in a kind of \nwondering terror, fearful of what this choosing should \nmean.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
