        "evidence_excerpt": "and cautious was he that never was he beaten from his \nJair—rather the enemy had to draw off sorely wounded \nand broken. Sometimes a party of vikings, tiring of \ntheir lawless and unsettled lives, would seize some land \nand build themselves houses and till the ground, and after \na time the country people would get used to the presence \nof the pirates turned farmers, and their daughters would \nmarry them, and in a few years there would be a little \ncolony of peaceful folk, and fields of waving corn where \nbefore had been the rushes and reeds of the wild river \nmeadows, sheltering naught but teal and snipe, otters \nand wolves. . |",
