      {
        "finding_id": "section-dcf40d88f658e3c5e753",
        "file": "bookofpirates0000gilb_bookofpirates0000gilb_djvu.txt",
        "title": "The book of pirates",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/bookofpirates0000gilb",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Gilbert, Henry, 1868-1937",
          "date": [
            "1916-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "1901bb10294e81f32d98c63cba8e03792d5ae5388c5c8aacfc5b904d67fab94d",
        "evidence_excerpt": "mar, ‘the Sea Devil,’ so baneful a power did he wield. \nEvery year he added to the number of his fleet, and every \nyear the toll he took of fine vessels, rich cargoes, and \ncaptives became greater. Hitherto the Spanish Govern- \nment had affected to despise the attacks of these wasps of \nBarbary, but at length the authorities sent one of their \ngreatest military leaders, Don Pedro Navarro, to root \nout the robbers’ nests. By this time great numbers of \npirates haunted the ports and harbours of the African \ncoast, but as yet they were not conscious of their power, \nand their forces had not been co-ordinated nor their \nstrongholds made defensive, as was to be the case a few \nyears afterward. Navarro, with a great fleet, put out \nto sea, and was able to take possession of the three chief \npirate lairs—Oran, Bujeya, and Algiers—without much \ndifficulty. He burnt many galleys and knocked the \ntowns to pieces. He made the Dey of Algiers promise \nto renounce piracy in future, and in order to put a rein \nupon the Algerians, he caused a strong fort, the Pefion \nde Alger (‘the Rock of Algiers’) to be built on a rocky \nspit of land at the entrance to the harbour, where, manned \nby a Spanish garrison, for long it stood, the muzzles of \nits cannon grinning at the town and the roadstead, ready \nto belch forth destruction upon any sea-hawk who ven- \ntured to enter or leave the port. For some years the \nMoors of Algiers restrained their predatory and revenge- \nful impulses, and the pirates elsewhere either became \nmerchants or took their galleys and offered them and \ntheir own services to the Grand Turk at Stamboul, who \nreceived them with open arms, as goodly reinforcements \nto his fleet.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/bookofpirates0000gilb",
          "source_file": "bookofpirates0000gilb_bookofpirates0000gilb_djvu.txt",
