      {
        "finding_id": "section-69be0cb43ad731615df1",
        "file": "bookofpirates0000gilb_bookofpirates0000gilb_djvu.txt",
        "title": "The book of pirates",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/bookofpirates0000gilb",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Gilbert, Henry, 1868-1937",
          "date": [
            "1916-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "1901bb10294e81f32d98c63cba8e03792d5ae5388c5c8aacfc5b904d67fab94d",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Teach, therefore, after having buried his booty in some \n-hiding-place, pretended to turn honest, and we may now \nimagine him as leading a roaring life ashore, a penitent \npirate enjoying the pardon of his Majesty, which was \ngranted to all sea-robbers on that coast who surrendered \nto the royal mercy within a certain time. It appears, \nhowever, that as a matter of fact he was anything but \na reformed robber, for, as the old record has it, his sub- \nmission was not done from any real reformation of \nmanner of thinking, “‘ but only to await a more favour- \nable opportunity to play the same game over again.” \nMeanwhile he cultivated the acquaintance of the Governor \nof the State, Charles Eden, and the Governor’s secretary, \na man named Knight, both of whom he found to be \nwilling tools, eager to share in any spoils which he might \ngain. , \nWe need not suppose that Blackbeard gave a thought \nto the seventeen men he had marooned on the desolate",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
