          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "1901bb10294e81f32d98c63cba8e03792d5ae5388c5c8aacfc5b904d67fab94d",
        "evidence_excerpt": "“Lieutenant Maynard came to an anchor, for the \nplace being shoal and the channel intricate there was \nno getting in where Teach lay that night; but in the \nmorning he weighed, and sent his boat ahead of the sloops \nto sound, and coming within gun-shot of the pirate, \nreceived his fire ; whereupon Maynard hoisted the King’s \ncolours and stood directly towards him with the best \nway that his sails and oars could make. Blackbeard cut \nhis cable, and endeavoured to make a running fight, \nkeeping a continual fire at his enemies with his guns. \nMr Maynard, not having any, kept up a constant fire \nwith small arms, while some'of his men laboured at their \noars. Ina little time Teach’s sloop ran aground, and Mr \nMaynard’s drawing more water than that of the pirate, \nhe could not come near him; so he anchored within half \ngun-shot of the enemy ; and in order to lighten his vessel, \nthat he might run him aboard, the lieutenant ordered all \nhis ballast to be thrown overboard and all his water \nbarrels to be staved in and then weighed and stood for \nhin aoe",
