      {
        "finding_id": "section-3d5d69f8dfd604b204b8",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "these  multiplied  themselves  to  the  tale  of  something \nlike  twenty -four,  the  conviction  was  strengthened \nthat  that  which  had  so  profoundly  interested  the \nwriter,  would  not  be  altogether  indifferent  to  others. \nFor  some  inscrutable  reason  the  deeds  of  sea-robbers \nhave  always  possessed  a  fascination  denied  to  those \nof  their  more  numerous  brethren  of  the  land  ;  and  in \nthe  case  of  the  Sea-wolves  of  the  sixteenth  century \nwe  are  dealing  with  the  very  aristocrats  of  the  pro- \nfession. Circumstances  over  which  they  had  no \ncontrol  flung  the  Moslem  population  of  Southern \nSpain  on  to  the  shores  of  Northern  Africa:  to \nrevenge  themselves  upon  the  Christian  foe  by \nwhom  this  expropriation  had  been  accomplished \nwas  natural  to  a  warrior  race ;  and  those  who \nheretofore  had  been  land-folk  pure  and  simple  took \nto  piracy  as  a  means  of  livehhood.  It  is  of  the \ndeeds  of  these  men  that  this  book  treats ;  of  their \nmarvellous  triumphs,  of  their  apparently  hopeless \ndefeats,  of  the  manner  in  which  they  audaciously \nmaintained  themselves  against  the  principalities \nand  the  powers  of  Christendom  always  hungering \nfor  their  destruction.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
