      {
        "finding_id": "section-113408b10a6fc3c4c32a",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "were  vowed  to  the  extermination  of  what  they, \non  their  side,  called  \"  the  infidel.\"  It  was  an  age \nof  iron,  when  inen  neither  gave  nor  expected \ngrace  for  the  misfortunes  which  might  befall  them \nin  the  warrior  life  which  they  led.  It  was  dis- \ntinguished by  many  gallant  feats  of  arms  on  both \nsides,  but  pity  formed  no  part  of  the  equipment \nof  the  fighting  man  bent  on  the  death  or  capture \nof  his,  enemy.  Honestly  and  sincerely  each  side \nbelieved  that  they  were  doing  the  service  of  the \nAlmighty  in  destroying  the  other  party  root  and \nbranch.  The  amount  of  human  misery  and \nsuffering  caused  by  the  rise  and  progress  of  the \nMoslem  corsairs  was  absolutely  incalculable  ;  the \nslavery  of  the  rower  in  the  galley  in  the  time  of \nwhich  we  speak  was  an  agony  so  dreadful  that  in \nthese  days  it  is  a  thing  which  seems  altogether \nincredible,  a  nightmare  of  horror  almost  impossible \neven  to  imagine.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
