          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Popes  and  emperors,  kings  and  princes,  found \nthemselves  compelled  to  form  leagues  against  these \nSea-wolves  who  devoured  the  substance  of  their \nsubjects,  and  great  expeditions  were  fitted  out  to \nfight  with  and  destroy  the  corsairs.  Had  Christen- \ndom been  united  no  doubt  the  object  would  have \nbeen  attained  ;  but,  as  will  be  seen  at  the  end  of  this \nchapter,  an  \"  Alliance  of  Christian  Princes  against \nthe  Tui'ks — which  generic  term  included  the  cor- \nsairs— was  not  always  used  in  the  manner  best \ncalculated  to  injure  those  common  enemies.",
