      {
        "finding_id": "section-477708c7331384f4fe51",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "had  no  desire  at  present  to  cultivate  the  further \nacquaintance  of  the  knights,  and  therefore  put  the \nwhole  width  of  the  Ionian  Sea  between  himself  and \nthem,  and  succeeded  in  taking  several  rich  prizes. \nHe  avoided  Mitylene  and  returned  to  Egypt, \nwintering  at  Alexandria.  It  may  here  be  remarked \nthat  the  corsairs,  as  a  rule,  regarded  the  winter  as \na  close  season,  as  in  those  early  days  the  mariner \ndid  not,  if  he  could  avoid  it,  risk  his  ship  by  sailing \nher  at  this  period  of  storm  and  tempest.  In  con- \nsequence there  was  nothing  to  tempt  the  pirates  to \nrange  the  seas  during  these  months,  and  if  they \nhad  had  a  successful  summer  and  autumn,  as  they \ngenerally  did,  they  could  well  aiFord  to  lay  up  and \nawait  the  coming  of  spring.",
