      {
        "finding_id": "section-5cc6e086ef3f4e2a1f91",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Berber  chieftain,  the  luckless  Selim  Eutemi,  and \ncaused  him  to  be  assassinated,  regarding  him  as \nbeing  responsible  for  the  failure.  The  Spanish \nchroniclers  relate,  with  some  wealth  of  detail,  how \nUruj  personally  fell  upon  SeUm  Eutemi,  when \nthat  chieftain  was  in  his  bath,  and  strangled  him \nwith  his  own  hands.  However  this  may  have  been, \nthe  Spanish  records  of  the  deeds  of  the  corsairs \ncannot  well  be  taken  au  pied  de  la  lettre ;  there \nis  no  doubt  that  Selim  was  murdered,  and  from \nthat  time  the  Berbers  recognised  that  he  who  had \ncome  to  help  was  now  remaining  to  plunder.  Uruj \nnow  established  himself  in  the  town,  and  set  to \nwork  making  raids  into  the  adjoining  country, \ncarrying  off  sheep,  cattle,  and  slaves.  For  the \nBerbers  this  was  a  true  awakening.  He  who  now \noppressed  them  had  come  in  the  guise  of  a \nchampion  to  assist  them  in  the  sack  and  plunder \nof  Navarro's  Tower ;  they  had  exchanged  King \nLog,  who  dwelt  securely  locked  up,  for  a  King \nStork  of  the  most  active  description.  Although \nwe  cannot  sympathise  with  such  people,  it  is  quite \npossible  to  understand  their  very  natural  annoy- \nance at  the  turn  which  things  had  taken,  and  it \ndoes  not  surprise  us  (in  this  age  of  \"  punic  faith  \") \nthat  a  conspiracy  was  set  on  foot  between  the \ndwellers  of  the  hinterland  and  the  Spaniards  of \nthe  fortress.",
