      {
        "finding_id": "section-107151b5f544ad03f5f9",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "sought — allies  to  assist  him  against  the  revolted \nVenalcadi  and  the  recalcitrant  Hassan.  Lurking \nin  the  neighbourhood  of  Bizerta,  he  discovered \nEl  Judeo  (the  Jew),  Cachidiablo  (Hunt  the  Devil), \nSalaerrez,  Tabas,  and  other  corsairs,  who  collec- \ntively composed  a  formidable  force.  These  were \nall  old  acquaintances  and  some  old  followers  of \nKheyr-ed-Din,  and  to  them  did  he  relate  the \npiteous  tale  of  the  cowardice  of  Venalcadi,  whom \nhe  accused  of  having  deserted  his  brother  Uruj \nin  his  direst  necessity,  thereby  causing  his  death ; \nthe  abominable  conduct  of  Hassan,  who  had  turned \nand  bitten  the  hand  that  fed  him.  With  tears  in \nhis  eyes  did  this  accomplished  actor  reluctantly \nreveal  the  base  ingratitude  of  which  he  had  been \nthe  recipient ;  so  much  did  he  contrive  to  work \nupon  the  feeUngs  of  his  auditors  that  they  one \nand  all  vowed  to  stand  by  him,  and  to  replace  him \nas  ruler  of  Algiers,  from  which  he  had  been  thrust \nby  men  whose  shameful  treachery  was  only  equalled \nby  their  ingratitude.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
