      {
        "finding_id": "section-0bc843581346dab42fb4",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "On  his  side  Kheyr-ed-Din  looked  with  longing \neyes  on  this  remnant  of  the  power  of  Spain  in \nAfrica.  Could  he  but  dislodge  Martin  de  Vargas, \nhe  had  the  whole  of  Northern  Africa  practically \nat  his  disposal ;  Algiers  would  then  be  really  his, \nto  fortify  for  all  time  against  the  inroads  of  his \nfoes.  He  was  master  by  land  and  sea,  the  time \nwas  propitious  ;  the  corsair  decided  that  the  hour \nhad  come.  He  had  seen  the  repulse  of  his  brother \nUruj,  none  knew  better  than  did  he  the  temper \nof  the  men  by  whom  the  Penon  was  held,  or  the \nvaliance  and  the  unswerving  fidelity  of  that  cabal- \nlero  of  Spain,  Martin  de  Vargas.  He  tried  to \ninduce  that  officer  to  surrender  to  him,  offering \nevery  inducement  to  the  Spanish  commander  to \ncome  to  terms.  He  was  met  with  a  haughty  re- \nfusal, couched  in  the  most  contemptuous  language. \nHe  tried  the  most  blood-curdling  threats,  which \nwere  no  empty  menaces,  as  his  adversary  well \nknew :  these  were  received  in  silence.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
