      {
        "finding_id": "section-324cb59d1759d8236b4c",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "had  placed  on  his  own  head  the  iron  crown  of \nLombardy  ;  his  viceroys  ruled  in  Naples  and  Sicily, \nhis  dukes  and  feudatories  in  Florence  and  Ferrara, \nin  Mantua  and  in  Milan  ;  there  was  no  more  Italy. \nAll  these  recent  acquisitions  had  been  rendered \npossible  by  the  defection  of  Andrea  Doria,  the \nGenoese  seaman,  from  Francis  I.  of  France  to  the \nside  of  the  Emperor.  From  henceforward  it  was \nagainst  this  modern  Caesar  that  Barbarossa  had  to \ncontend ;  the  monarch  under  whose  banner \nswarmed  the  terrible  Schwartz-Reiters  of  Germany, \nfor  whose  honour  marched  the  incomparable  in- \nfantry of  Spain,  for  whom  the  fleets  of  the  gallant \nGenoese  sailed  in  battle-array  under  the  orders  of \nthe  greatest  admiral  of  the  day,  Andrea  Doria. \nAll  these  disciplined  legions  of  Christendom  were \narrayed  against  the  corsair  king  ;  banded  together \nfor  the  destruction  of  that  daring  pirate  whose  flag \nfloated  in  insolent  triumph  above  the  white  walls \nof  Algiers.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
