      {
        "finding_id": "section-cb15b146d97df6459c2c",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "On  that  stage,  which  was  the  blue  waters  of  the \ntideless  sea,  we  shall,  from  this  time  forward,  watch \nthe  fortunes  of  those  two  great  sea-captains, \nAndrea  Doria  and  Kheyr-ed-Din  Barbarossa. \nWith  them  the  ebb  and  flow  of  conquest  and \ndefeat  alternated.  Great  as  was  the  one,  it  cannot \nbe  said  that  he  was  greater  than  the  other ;  but \nwhen  the  supreme  arbitrament  was  within  the \ngrasp  of  both,  as  it  was  at  the  naval  battle  of \nPrevesa,  neither  the  Christian  admiral  nor  the \nMoslem  corsair  would  reach  out  his  hand  and \ngrasp  the  nettle  of  his  fate.  Hesitation  at  this \nmoment,  when,  in  the  fulness  of  time,  the  rivals \nstood  face  to  face  with  arms  in  their  hands,  was \nthe  last  thing  that  would  have  been  expected  of \nsuch  dauntless  warriors,  such  born  leaders  of  men  I \nand  the  battle  of  Prevesa  presents  a  psychological \nproblem  of  the  most  baffling  and  perplexing  de- \nscription. We  are,  however,  anticipating  events \nwhich  will  fall  into  their  proper  sequence  as  we \nproceed.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
