      {
        "finding_id": "section-6368c311c0ce5f7bbe33",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The  strain  must  have  been  great,  the  effort \nenormous,  and  he  knew,  as  he  was  bound  to  know, \nthat  his  coming  had  unloosed  jealousies  and  heart- \nsearchings  innumerable,  with  which  he  could  not \ndeal  in  the  usual  drastic  fashion  common  to  him. \nThe  winter  was  coming  on,  which  was,  as  we \nhave  before  remarked,  very  much  of  a  close  season \nboth  for  the  pirate  and  the  honest  merchant  sea- \nman. In  consequence  there  was  not  very  much \nchance  against  the  foes  of  Soliman  for  the  present. \nWhen  that  opportunity  offered  he  promised  him- \nself that  the  courtiers  and  the  soldiers  of  the  Grand \nTurk  would  very  soon  discover  that  the  fame  of \nKheyr-ed-Din  Barbarossa  was  no  empty  matter, \nand  that  there  existed  no  seaman  in  all  the  Otto- \nman dominions  with  whom  they  could  compare  the \n\"  African  pirate,\"  as  he  had  reason  to  believe  that \nhe  was  scornfully  called  behind  his  back.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
