      {
        "finding_id": "section-a3386a60459029d7de1a",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "begun  or  finished  at  the  time  of  Paul  III.,  have \nnothing  to  do  with  the  sack  of  1527,  ^V^ith  the \nConn^table  de  Bourbon,  or  with  the  Emperor \nCharles  V.  All  the  bastions,  that  of  the  Belvedere \nexcepted,  point  towards  the  sea-coast,  which  was \nperpetually  harried  and  terrified  by  Turkish  or \nBarbary  pirates.  These  would  appear  with  lightning- \nlike rapidity  in  more  than  one  place  at  a  time,  and \ncarry  off  as  many  unfortunate  men,  women,  and \nchildren  as  they  could  collect.  .  .  .  To  prevent \nthe  recurrence  of  such  disasters  the  sea- coast  was \nlined  with  watch-towers,  the  guns  of  which  could \nwarn  the  peasants  of  the  approach  of  suspicious \nvessels.\"",
        "years_in_scope": [],
