      {
        "finding_id": "section-ebae24b0c1c1a0e6b3d6",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Around  him  the  Reis,  or  captains  of  the  Moslem \ngalleys,  clamorously  demanded  that  he  should  take \nprecautions  against  a  land-attack.  It  was  true  that \nthe  raid  which  had  been  made  by  Grimani  had \nbeen  easily  repulsed,  but  in  present  circumstances \nthere  was  no  question  of  a  mere  raid,  as,  should  the \nChristian  admiral  so  decide,  he  could  land  twenty \nthousand  men.  Sinan  Reis,  an  old  Osmanli  warrior, \nfurious  with  jealousy  that  the  chief  command \nshould  be  in  the  hands  of  a  corsair,  sustained  his \nopinion  in  a  manner  which  augured  ill  for  the \nhearty  co-operation  of  all  the  Turkish  forces. \nSinan  was  just  one  of  those  blindly  valiant  fighters \nfrom  whom  the  politic  Ibrahim  had  desired  to \ndeliver  his  master  when  he  had  urged  the  appoint- \nment of  Kheyr-ed-Din :  brave  as  a  lion,  keen  as",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
