      {
        "finding_id": "section-15bc0e59ba95bf0fe489",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Promptly,  hurriedly,  but  efficiently,  the  corsair \norganised  his  new  possession  :  such  laws  as  he \ndecreed  did  not  err  on  the  side  of  tenderness \ntowards  a  people  so  ungrateful  as  to  have  refused \nhis  protection  in  the  first  instance,  and  who  had \nonly  accepted  the  gift  at  the  point  of  the  sword. \nHis  nephew  Aisa,  a  man  young  in  years  but  a \npast-graduate  in  the  school  of  his  terrible  uncle, \nwas  left  in  charge,  while  Dragut  himself  sailed \nonce  more  with  his  fleet,  for,  as  it  is  put  by  the \nSpanish  historian  Marmol,  \"  truly  the  sea  was  his \nelement.\"",
