      {
        "finding_id": "section-ccb5f4b7c677b6307e8b",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "expression  of  King  Philip  II.  of  Spain ;  the  same \nidea  might '  have  crossed  the  mind  of  Doria  on \nthis  memorable  occasion.  He  had  only  to  wait ; \nthe  longer  he  waited  the  more  secure  he  would  be \nof  success,  the  more  certain  would  he  be  of  the \ncomplete  undoing  of  his  enemy.  But  even  yet \nthe  admiral  did  not  know  the  man  to  whom  he \nwas  opposed ;  in  all  the  years  in  which  he  had \ndone  battle  against  Dragut,  he  had  never  gauged \nthe  limitless  resource  and  calculated  audacity  of \nthis  lineal  successor  of  Kheyr-ed-Din  Barbarossa. \nWhile  the  admiral  had  been  sending  his  despatches, \nand  idly  watching  that  which  he  considered  to  be \nthe  futile  construction  of  earthworks  on  the  shore \nat  the  Bocca  de  Cantara,  his  enemy  was  preparing \nfor  him  that  surprise  which  was  shortly  afterwards \nto  make  of  him  the  laughing-stock  of  the  whole  of \nEurope.  Dragut  was  in  a  trap,  and  he  was  quite \naware  of  the  fact ;  by  way  of  the  Bocca  de  Cantara \nescape  was  impossible,  and  neither  a  tame  surrender \nnor  complete  annihilation  was  by  any  means  to  the \ntaste  of  the  pirate  leader.  Had  Doria  gone  in  and \nattacked  at  once,  the  fate  of  the  corsair  had  been \nsealed  ;  the  policy  of  delay  adopted  by  the  Christian \nadmiral  was  his  salvation,",
