        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The  abuse  of  the  Knights  fell  upon  sympathetic \nears ;  in  his  early  days  Soliman  the  Magnificent \nhad  expelled  the  Knights  from  Rhodes  ;  since  then \nCharles  V.  had  given  them  the  islands  of  Malta \nand  Gozo,  and  the  town  of  Tripoli  in  Barbary  as \ntheir  abiding  place  ;  from  Malta  they  had  never \nceased  their  warfare  against  the  corsairs,  and \nincidentally  against  the  Sultan  and  his  subjects. \nTherefore,  in  this  year  1551,  Soliman  ordained \nthat  an  expedition  should  be  prepared  with  the \nobject  of  crushing  once  and  for  all  these  troublers \nof  the  peace  of  Islam.  The  preparations  were  on \nso  large  a  scale  that  very  soon  it  became  noised \nabroad  in  Europe  that  something  really  serious  was \nin  the  wind :  in    Constantinople,   however,  men",
