      {
        "finding_id": "section-495e009981e7ce70d228",
        "file": "greatpiratesstor0000jose_greatpiratesstor0000jose_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Great pirate stories. Second series",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/greatpiratesstor0000jose",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "1925-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "97e06b715ec15879e9da76b05cd5250edc5df0b287c0736c121fbebff2f7fc41",
        "evidence_excerpt": "as the West Indies, have been their haunts for cen- \nturies; and vessels navigating the Atlantic and Indian \nOceans, are often captured by them, the passengers \nand crew murdered, the money and most valuable part \nof the cargo plundered, the vessel destroyed, thus ob- \nliterating all trace of their unhappy fate, and leaving \nfriends and relatives to mourn their loss from the in- \nclemencies of the elements, when they were butchered \nin cold blood by their fellow men, who by practically \nadopting the maxim that “dead men tell no tales,” \nenable themselves to pursue their diabolical career with \nimpunity. The pirate is truly fond of women and \nwine, and when not engaged in robbing, keeps mad- \ndened with intoxicating liquors, and passes his time in \ndebauchery, singing old songs with choruses like",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
