      {
        "finding_id": "section-03869c76a46006951e17",
        "file": "greatpiratesstor0000jose_greatpiratesstor0000jose_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Great pirate stories. Second series",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/greatpiratesstor0000jose",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "1925-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "97e06b715ec15879e9da76b05cd5250edc5df0b287c0736c121fbebff2f7fc41",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Soon after Bunch began to tell what bright arms \nthey had on board their sloop; upon which, one of \nAugur’s men handed up some of their cutlasses which \nhad been cleaned that day. Among them was Mr. \nCarr’s silver-hilted sword. Bunch seemed to admire \nthe sword, and asked whose it was? Mr. Carr made \nanswer, it belonged to him. Bunch replied it was \na very handsome one, and drawing it out, marched \nabout the poop, flourishing it over his head, and telling \nMr. Carr he would return it to him when he had \ndone with it. At the same time he began to vapour \nagain, and to boast of his former piracies, and coming \nnear Mr. Carr, struck him with the sword. ‘Turnley \nbid him take care what he did, for Mr. Carr would \nnot take such usage. As they were disputing upon \nthis matter, Dennis Mackarthy stole off, and, with \nsome of his associates, seized upon the great cabin, \nwhere all the arms lay. At the same time several \nof the men began to sing a song with these words. \nDid you not promise me, that you would marry me—",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
