      {
        "finding_id": "section-f254e2f0da012876091e",
        "file": "greatpiratesstor0000jose_greatpiratesstor0000jose_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Great pirate stories. Second series",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/greatpiratesstor0000jose",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "1925-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "97e06b715ec15879e9da76b05cd5250edc5df0b287c0736c121fbebff2f7fc41",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The Malay piratical proas are from six to eight \ntons burden, and run from six to eight fathoms in \nlength. ‘They carry from one to two small guns, with \ncommonly four swivels or rantakas to each side, and \na crew of from twenty to thirty men. When they \nengage, they put up a strong bulwark of thick plank; \nthe Illanoon proas are much larger and more formid- \nable, and commonly carry from four to six guns, and \na proportionable number of swivels, and have not un- \nfrequently a double bulwark covered with buffalo \nhides; their crews consist of from forty to eighty men. \nBoth, of course, are provided with spears, krisses, and \nas many fire arms as they can procure. ‘Their modes \nof attack are cautious and cowardly, for plunder and \nnot fame is their object. They lie concealed under \nthe land, until they find a fit object and opportunity. \nThe time chosen is when a vessel runs aground, or is \nbecalmed, in the interval between the land and sea \nbreezes. A vessel underway is seldom or never at- \ntacked. Several of the marauders attack together, \nand station themselves under the bows and quarters",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
