      {
        "finding_id": "section-78ae965642d8c6b23580",
        "file": "greatpiratesstor0000jose_greatpiratesstor0000jose_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Great pirate stories. Second series",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/greatpiratesstor0000jose",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "1925-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "97e06b715ec15879e9da76b05cd5250edc5df0b287c0736c121fbebff2f7fc41",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Whatever else Smith had in his character, he was \ncertainly extremely shrewd and cautious, and he re- \nplied in a non-committal answer to the effect that the \n“windward” passage might prolong the voyage but \nthat the “leeward” one would expose the ship to the \nrisk of being plundered by the pirates, which in those \ndays were far from rare. Lumsden weighed the pros \nand cons in his mind, and at last resolved to choose \nthe “leeward” passage. About two o'clock one after- \nnoon Smith was pacing up and down deck when he \nsuddenly espied a schooner of a very suspicious ap- \npearance standing out from the land. Not quite \nhappy as to her character, he then went aloft with his \ntelescope and examined her closely. In the case of a \nman of his sea experience it did not take long for \nhim to realise that the schooner was a pirate-ship. \nLumsden was below at the time, so Smith called him",
        "years_in_scope": [],
