      {
        "finding_id": "section-af92afe3e271297f8608",
        "file": "greatpiratesstor0000jose_greatpiratesstor0000jose_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Great pirate stories. Second series",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/greatpiratesstor0000jose",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "1925-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "97e06b715ec15879e9da76b05cd5250edc5df0b287c0736c121fbebff2f7fc41",
        "evidence_excerpt": "1] was much amused when I returned to England, at a penny \nillustrated newspaper which had been sent to my address, soon after \nthis piratical affair had taken place. Amidst any amount of smoke \nand fire, men mounted on ardent steeds are represented galloping \nabout in all directions, armed with long spears, shields, and battle- \naxes,—these are the pirates. Other men, with helmets on, and \nclothed in complete armour, are closely engagd with these mounted \nwarriors; some are in the act of springing on shore from numerous \nboats, which are just discernible amidst the fire, smoke, and con- \nfusion,—these represent the gallant British tars, the Opposum’s crew. \nIt must have been a fertile imagination that got all this together, to \nshow what piracy in China was like!",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
