      {
        "finding_id": "section-cbf1d74d78821127c274",
        "file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-Wolves of the Mediterranean: The Grand Period of the Moslem Corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In the territory known to the Romans as Byza- \ncena, which stretched from Algiers to the confines \nof Tripoli, there was reigning at this period one \nAbu-Abd-Allah-Mahomed, a Berber Moslem of \nthe dynasty of Hafsit. Between this dignitary \nand Genoa a treaty of commerce had been ar- \nranged and signed. But treaties on the shores \nof the Mediterranean were capable of very elastic \ninterpretation; they never reckoned with the \ncorsairs, and these latter were in the habit of \nintruding themselves everywhere, and upsetting \nthe most carefully laid plans. Curtogali, a corsair \nwho had collected a great following, was now a \npower with which to reckon, and high in the \nfavour of the Grand Turk at Constantinople. \nThis robber presented himself at Bizerta—one of \nthe ports of Abd-Allah-Mahomed—with a squad- \nron of thirty ships, and demanded hospitality. As \nCurtogali disposed of thirty ships and some six \nthousand fighting men it would probably have \nbeen impossible for Abd-Allah to have refused \nhis request in any case; but he was far from \nwishing to do so, as, by a convenient interpretation \nof the Koran, the pirate had to deliver up one- \nfifth part of all the booty which he reft from \nthe Christians to the ruler of the country in whose \nharbours he sheltered. There was no place so \nconvenient for the purposes of the pirate as Bizerta : \nfrom here he could strike at Sicily, at the Balearic \nIslands, at Rome, Naples, Tuscany, and Liguria,",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
          "source_file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 72418,
          "end_char": 73876,
          "start_line": 2064,
          "end_line": 2093,
          "printed_page": null,
          "scan_indexes_0_based": [],
          "pdf_file": null,
          "pdf_reference_base": "LANE_ROOT",
          "original_inspected": false
        },
        "reported_cited_pages": [],
        "decision": "REVIEW_CONTEXT",
        "reason": "THEMATIC_SOURCE_PASSAGE_NOT_VERIFIED_BASE",
        "extraction_method": "DETERMINISTIC_THEMATIC_PASSAGE_READING",
