        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "It was well for the corsair to make as many \nfriends as possible, as among his enemies he counted \nall the kings of Christendom ; and, looking back \non his career, it seems but little short of a miracle \nthat he was not crushed out of existence, not once \nbut a hundred times. But, as has been said already, \nthe root of true statesmanship was in Kheyr-ed- \nDin. He watched with eager eye the quarrels of \nthe great kings on the continent of Europe; he \nsaw his life-long rival at sea, the greatest of all \nChristian mariners, Andrea Doria, the Genoese \nadmiral, transfer his allegiance from the French \nKing Francis I. to the Emperor Charles V. He \nnoted and took full advantage of the perpetual \nsquabbles between the Genoese and Venetian",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
