      {
        "finding_id": "section-f1922dd5826290b63665",
        "file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-Wolves of the Mediterranean: The Grand Period of the Moslem Corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "On that stage, which was the blue waters of the \ntideless sea, we shall, from this time forward, watch \nthe fortunes of those two great  sea-captains, \nAndrea Doria and Kheyr-ed-Din Barbarossa. \nWith them the ebb and flow of conquest and \ndefeat alternated. Great as was the one, it cannot \nbe said that he was greater than the other; but \nwhen the supreme arbitrament was within the \ngrasp of both, as it was at the naval battle of \nPrevesa, neither the Christian admiral nor the \nMoslem corsair would reach out his hand and \ngrasp the nettle of his fate. Hesitation at this \nmoment, when, in the fulness of time, the rivals \nstood face to face with arms in their hands, was \nthe last thing that would have been expected of \nsuch dauntless warriors, such born leaders of men! \nand the battle of Prevesa presents a psychological \nproblem of the most baffling and perplexing de- \nscription. We are, however, anticipating events \nwhich will fall into their proper sequence as we \nproceed.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
