      {
        "finding_id": "section-be66b55a19407081a341",
        "file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-Wolves of the Mediterranean: The Grand Period of the Moslem Corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The Knights then, headed by the Grand Master, \ntook themselves in procession to the church. Here \nthey confessed and received the sacrament. ‘“ They \nwent out from thence as men who had received a \nnew birth.” The Knights, we are then told, tenderly \nembraced one another in all solemnity ; vowing to \nshed the last drop of their blood in defence of \ntheir religion and its holy altars. It was in this \nlofty frame of mind that the Knights of Malta \nawaited the coming of their hereditary foe. Into \nthe hearts and minds of these gallant gentlemen of \nthe best blood in the world the Grand Master had \ninstilled some leaven of the greatness by which he \nhimself was inspired. When belief is so whole- \nhearted as it was in the case of La Valette ; when it \nis allied to a genius for war, and a supreme gift for \nthe inspiration of others, then that man and the \nforce which he commands are as near to invincibility \nas it is permitted to fallible human beings to attain. \nThere were two things in which the Knights were \nsupremely fortunate on this occasion: the first was \nthat they had La Valette as Grand Master, the \nsecond that Dragut was not in supreme command \nof the Turks, and that the siege had opened before \nhe arrived upon the scene. In this expedition, as \nin previous ones, the Turkish commanders had \norders to attempt nothing really important without",
