          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "There were three principal conspirators—the \nAlcaid Bengabara, General of the Cavalry, the \nAlcaid Botaybo, and the Alcaid Alcadaar. Alli, \nhowever, was too shrewd a man to move until he \nhad satisfied himself by reports from his own ad- \nherents; he, therefore, awaited the result of in- \nvestigations made by spies from Algiers. At last, \nin the beginning of the year 1569, when the offers \nfrom the Alcaids had been three times renewed \nand the Basha was assured that the people in \n‘Tunis were sincere in their offer to him of the \nsovereignty of the kingdom—which they begged \nhim to conquer and hold in the name of the \nOttoman Empire—the ex-galley-slave no longer \nhesitated. He left Algiers in the month of \nOctober, leaving that city in charge of one Mami \nCorso, a fellow renegado. Unlike Dragut, who \nwould have gone by sea, he set out by land with \nsome five thousand corsairs and renegadoes. On \nthe way he was reinforced by some six thousand \ncavalry of the wild tribes of the hinterland, then",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
