      {
        "finding_id": "section-36238ccd2fa58908f45c",
        "file": "journalofvoyagei00digbiala_journalofvoyagei00digbiala_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Journal of a voyage into the Mediterranean",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/journalofvoyagei00digbiala",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Digby, Kenelm, Sir, 1603-1665",
            "Bruce, John, 1802-1869"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1868-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "d2defe6ff388b631877f74b1fb4b681f503d63d34637330ce13bec173be3c0b1",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In  the  meantime,  mere  nopeless  ruin  seemed  to  have  overtaken  the \nother  branch  of  the  family.  Kenelm  Digby  died  in  1590  ;  his  son, \na  Sir  Everard,  the  favourite  Christian  name  of  the  family,*  followed \nhim  two  years  afterwards,  leaving  his  eldest  son,  another  Everard, \nat  the  dangerous  age  of  eleven.  The  boy  inherited  even  more  than \nthe  good  looks  which  belonged  to  all  his  house.  He  grew  up  to  be \nesteemed  the  handsomest  man  in  England.  He  was  knighted  in \n1603,  and  made  a  great  match  in  marriage  with  Mary,  daughter \nand  one  of  the  co-heiresses  of  William  Mulsho  of  Gayhurst,  or,  as \nit  was  then  called,  Gotehurst,  in  the  county  of  Buckingham.  On \nhis  marriage  he  removed  from  the  more  distant  Drystoke  to  the \npleasant,  or,  as  the  poet  Cowper  designates  it,  the  \"happy,\"  situa- \ntion of  Gayhurst.  But  during  the  few  years  of  his  occupancy \nGayhurst,  however  agreeable  its  commanding  site,  must  have  been \nanything  rather  than  a  happy  place.  Sir  Everard  probably  came \ninto  his  wife's  religion  as  well  as  her  property ;  at  any  event  he  was \na  Roman  Catholic.  Enthusiastically  anxious  to  promote  what  he \ndeemed  the  interests  of  his  Church,  he  entered,  when  little  more \nthan  a  boy,  into  the  intrigues  and  conspiracies  of  the  day.  Tradition \ntells  of  secret  chambers  constructed  by  him  at  Gayhurst  for  conceal- \nment of  priests,  and  of  meetings  held  there  of  persons  unknown,  and \nfor  purposes  not  disclosed,  and  therefore  deemed  culpable.  Once \non  this  dangerous  track,  the  love  he  bore  to  Catesby,  who  exercised \na  singular  fascination  over  all  his  friends,  not  merely  kept  the  shal- \nlow and  impetuous  Sir  Everard  steady,  but  plunged  him,  at  the  age \nof  twenty-four,  into  the  very  depths  of  the  Gunpowder  Treason.",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1603
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
