        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Only from the late 1970s did scholars more consistently begin to ex- \nplore what Europeans called piracy from non-European perspectives. Two \nmonographs that focused on Southeast Asia were ground-breaking in this \nrespect, Carl Trocki's Prince of Pirates (1979) and James Warren's The Sulu \nZone (1983). In recent decades, piracy and maritime raiding in other parts of \nAsia have also attracted attention from scholars working with manuscript \nsources in Asian archives, including in Muhammad al-Qasimi in the Persian",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
