      {
        "finding_id": "section-61f2c48b411d61f39f96",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Leaving aside for the moment the stylization of Cusack as a universal bogey- \nman, one could ask what the decisive reason was for his condemnation \nand execution as a pirate. He and his crew had without doubt repeatedly \nviolated the existing law of prize and the customary rules of most countries \non the seizure of booty.^5 The manipulation of a letter of marque was just \nas impermissible as the setting adrift of the captured crews on the open \nsea. In addition, the legality of the seizure of goods from the ships he had",
