        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Here, Locke invoked the image, \nalready well-established in European political thought, ofthe pirate as the \nincarnation of coercive, violent power without any title or justification. \nThe pirate was the direct opposite of the legitimate ruler who upheld the \nlaws, defended property, and served justice on malefactors.3 By arguing so, \nLocke steered a familiar course within the channel markers of European \npolitical thought, but his course soon took him into less familiar waters.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
