      {
        "finding_id": "section-669ec729c60eaa07aef8",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Even as representations of territorial control featured increasingly in \nmeasures of where high seas lay and what constituted piracy, legal history \nreveals a counterpoint to colonial denials of native states’ control over \ncoasts. After all, ifsuch coastal sovereignty were not recognized by would-be \ncolonizers, their offshore waters would essentially be rendered “empty” \nspace, whether for colonial appropriation or simply the rejection of native \nstates’ sovereignty. Maps, which formed a quasi-legal means to reconstruct \nthe property histories of new colonial possessions, legitimized the spread of \ncolonial power.® Yet, even as the work of colonial cartographers supported \nterritorial myths of dominion, in certain legal contexts, particular coasts \nwere acknowledged to be part of independent native realms. The remarks of \ncolonial officials in this regard actually deflate myths of colonial territorial \ncontrol, demonstrating, for instance, that courts of every level in the lesser",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
