      {
        "finding_id": "section-50bc28a4f9ada5725ebf",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "political activism and piracy. In the same year, Jarannika broke with the \nVOC by taking some Tamboran people aboard and sailing his flotilla to \nManggarai in Flores, an area that was contested between Gowa and Bima \nand where the Dutch had nothing at all to say. The King of Gowa, as a Dutch \nvassal, tried to call him back but was conveniently ignored - in fact, the \nDutch suspected that the king was not serious in his efforts. The year after \nthis, Jarannika and Pamolikang sought refuge in Selaparang in Lombok, in \nspite of the previous enmity with the Balinese. The Dutch heard a rumour \nto the effect that Jarannika had been captured by his hosts, since he had \nboarded a vessel belonging to the Balinese ruler, and sincerely hoped that \nthis would be true, “as he has deserved death, if only because of his latest \nwork in the kingdom of Sumbawa, where he has pillaged four villages.\"5",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
