      {
        "finding_id": "section-2a72fd571f444ce49adc",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Finally, it should be recalled that the two types of piracy had a common \nroot. When the Dutch Company officials complained about the illicit \nacts ofthe Bugis-Makassar seafarers (and they frequently did), they were \noblivious of the fact that they themselves had let the beasts out ofthe cage. \nLeonard Andaya and Kathy Wellen have described the enormous disruption \nand devastation brought about by the Makassar War.5? Aristocrats lost \ntheir old lands and positions, while ordinary families were faced with \nstarvation or large-scale violence. In these unsettled times, piracy was \na way to fight and survive for another day. The dilemma is known from",
        "years_in_scope": [],
