      {
        "finding_id": "section-65e9353767e1012945af",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "was, admittedly, more nuanced than earlier characterizations. As Resident \nof Baroda, charged with the responsibility of pacifying local society, his \napproach was political but, unlike his other colleagues in the Bombay \nCouncil, Walker was keen to contextualize predation and to draw important \ndistinctions between small states that used predation as a political resource \nand groups that were accustomed to raiding as a means of livelihood. He \nwas also emphatic in identifying the burden of Company regulations, of \nthe political uncertainty and conflict that had ravaged the region forcing \nchiefs and communities to turn to piracy. He was insistent on abandoning \nindemnification claims that simply added more pressure and spiked up \nthe possibility of escalating piracy; instead, he wished to bring pirate chiefs \nto a formal agreement that would bind them to maintain their part of the \nbargain, albeit with some concessions.",
