      {
        "finding_id": "section-de64298836a9a02c8b45",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In the sixteenth century, however, the texts' authors begin replacing the \nsea-robber appellation with levend and korsan, and the Venetian Italian \ntranslations invariably rendered these as “leventi et corsari.\"? The changes \nin vocabulary were just one response to the dramatic developments in the \nMediterranean maritime landscape, which in the half-century after 1482 had \nwitnessed the Ottomans' gradual dismemberment and absorption of most \nof the Venetian stato da mar and the rise of corsair-led imperial fleets on \nboth sides ofthe Mediterranean.? Petty local piracy remained a problem, \nand the influx of well-armed English and Dutch broadside sailing vessels \ninto the Mediterranean beginning in the 1580s was a new and significant \nsource of danger to, as well as of competition for, both Ottoman and Vene- \ntian merchant shipping.'? But it was the unrestrained rise of corsairing \nin North Africa, Malta, and later Livorno that led to heightened tensions \nand increased the opportunities for conflict, with both sides considering \nthe other responsible for restraining their co-religionists. While Venice \nclamoured for the Ottoman admiralty to clamp down on North African \ncorsairing and petitioned for the release of Venetian captives and the return \nof goods illegally seized by Ottoman-aligned corsairs, Istanbul complained \nrepeatedly about Venice's failure to interdict the Maltese and other Catholic \ncorsairs, including the Uskoks in Dalmatia, who stopped in or traversed",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
