        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "would subsequently defend their raids against the Venetians with the secular \njustification of retaliation and reprisal, since the Venetians were proactive \nin their defence against the corsairs (as was their right according to their \ntreaty) and were famously uncompromising to those they captured, who they \nfrequently executed on the spot. In response, Ottoman authorities turned \nagain to the language of Islamic law, not in support of raiding the “enemy \ninfidels,” but as an explanation for why the corsairs could not, dispatching \nfurther fatwas to North Africa asserting the primacy ofthe sultan and the \nnecessity of securing his permission to raid.?°",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
