      {
        "finding_id": "section-e8c921699300a8809dbf",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Yet, calls for North Africa's corsairs to observe the sultans’ ahdnames had \nbeen dispatched with some regularity since the 1580s, as Venetian, French, \nand English vessels came under increasing attack, and Istanbul's coercive \ncapacity in the capitals of corsairing had only declined since then. Several \ndecades of political, military, and fiscal crisis weakened the financial and \nadministrative links to the North African provinces, which became virtually \nself-governing in the aftermath of provincial reorganization in 1587, while \nthe Algerian and Tunisian corsairs with roots in the Ottoman Aegean and \nAdriatic whom Mustafa Ali had celebrated, like Hayreddin Barbarossa and \nhis acolytes, had increasingly come to be replaced by European renegades \n— converted former captives, unemployed English and Dutch privateers, \nand entrepreneurial opportunists — who paid lip service to the old ideals \n(particularly the ancient and enduring hostility toward Spain) but had little \nconnection and less loyalty to the distant Ottoman dynasty.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
