      {
        "finding_id": "section-eb95e93eab2774a22a89",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Although late imperial China’s rule of law and sovereignty were, like \nEurope’s, lumpy and uneven, even so the Qing Empire developed quite \ndifferently. Unlike European states which expanded their empires across \nthe entire globe, Chinese states never attempted to extend their empires \nacross the oceans; empire-building was always internal and territorial across \ncontiguous areas of the continent (with the exceptions of Taiwan and Hainan \nislands, but they too were contiguous areas). Formally, both the Ming and \nQing governments wanted to control and confine all outside contacts with \nrigid restrictions on maritime trade and communication, and at times they \neven completely banned their subjects from going out to sea or leaving China. \nInformally, however, private individuals and families — largely merchants, \nsmugglers, pirates, and dissidents — extended the scope of China's activities \nfar beyond its shores to fully participate in the nascent world system.3 \nWhereas European empires extended their legal regimes and chased \nafter pirates across the globe, China's imperial governments treated pirates",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
