      {
        "finding_id": "section-d4016623e5c014594c3d",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "sovereignty of the imperium. With tens of thousands of followers under \ntheir command, in 1801 leading pirate chiefs issued a proclamation directly \nchallenging Qing rule: ^We [pirates] should follow Heaven's will and rise \nup to restore the Ming dynasty. [...] On May 1, 1801, the following order has \nbeen distributed to our brothers on the sea in Guangdong and Guangxi: we \nwill gather together all the ships on April 15, 1802, and move to conquer the \ntwo provinces.\" Pirates further infringed on the prerogatives of authority by \nmimicking the central government's administrative functions by operating \ntheir own tax offices, employing a bureaucracy of specialized personnel, \nmanufacturing gunpowder and weapons, and organizing war fleets into \n\"banners\" (qi) in direct imitation of the Qing state.”° Outside the purview of \nthe government and its laws, pirates devised their own autonomous laws to \nmaintain order among themselves. As outlaws they were at liberty to adopt \nany form of organization they wished. They opted to bind themselves with \n“compacts” (yue) that defined gangs as cohesive, self-governing bodies, \ndetailed the allocation of booty, and the enforcement of discipline. One \nwritten compact, composed and signed by seven confederation chieftains \nin 1805, consisted of eight regulations designed to control and keep harmony \namong the various pirate gangs. The pact was mutually binding on all \nseven groups of pirates, whether large or small, weak or strong. Another \ncompact, promulgated in 1807, stipulated procedures for settling disputes, \nguaranteed the equitable distribution of booty through a “common chest,” \nand protected women from sexual abuse.”’ As outlaws they lived by their \nown rules and owed loyalty to no state.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
