          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Although in 1809 the pirates were at the height of their power and exer- \ncised hegemonic control over maritime China, within a year they had utterly \ncollapsed.?? As previously in the early Qing, the state's inability to militar- \nily eradicate piracy inevitably forced officials to adopt an “appeasement” \n(zhaoan) policy whereby pirates who surrendered received generous pardons \nand monetary rewards. Pirate leaders, such as Guo Podai and Zhang Bao, \nwere rewarded naval commissions and dispatched to attack other pirates; \nlarge numbers of rank-and-file pirates were resettled in inland frontiers",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
