      {
        "finding_id": "section-0afe8ff521173663787d",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "land-based interpretations of piracy, a point made by Peter Shapinsky.5 \nShapinsky has shown that feudal Japanese lords increased their economic \nand political power by sponsoring piratical activities since the fourteenth \ncentury. Another crucial point is that piracy cannot be understood in- \ndependently of early modern political economies or as a self-sufficient \nor autonomous phenomenon. In his work on early modern piracy in the \nChina Seas, historian Robert J. Antony has stressed the importance of the \nclandestine economy as a by-product of piracy, providing many illuminating \nexamples of flourishing black markets in late imperial China. Throughout \nhistory, any larger maritime movement needed services and infrastructure \nprovided by the coastal populations, port communities and strategically \nlocated islands.? The complicated socio-economic web of supply and demand \nthus provided plenty of room for collaboration between alleged enemy \ngroups.",
