      {
        "finding_id": "section-9b2518ff094fcf3cfde4",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Colonial Spanish accounts describe how Japanese pirates (gente con \npoderosa armada, corsario or gente de mar?) carried out their operations \nfrom a settlement in Cagayan on their northern edge of Luzon in the \n1580s.^^ Cagayan was also the point of entry were Spanish missionaries and \nofficials feared the invasion of the Japanese commissioned by Toyotomi \nHideyoshi (1537—1598).* In 1592, news arrived in Manila that if the Japanese \nwere to land in Cagayan (via Taiwan or Ryukyu), the natives of Cagayan \nwould deliver the Spaniards to them.*° A Chinese Christian based in \nHirado (a small island in Kyushu not far from the above-mentioned pirate \nhubs Tsushima and Goto, which turned into a thriving international \nport with significant “piratical” Chinese and European settlements in \nthe second half of the sixteenth century*?) declared that even pilots of \nregular mercantile vessels used to stop at Cagayan to plunder on their",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
