      {
        "finding_id": "section-7be2f38583ff3934dcf1",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "After being rebuffed by the ruler of Japan, Acuña repeated the anti-Dutch \nmantra in a letter sent to Southern China in 1606. Thanking the Viceroy of \nFujian for his punishment of joint Sino-Dutch piratical operations along \nthe Fujianese coast, he remarked that the “Dutch are not friends of the \nCastilians, but bitter enemies; for, although they are vassals of the king \nof the Hespafias, my sovereign, they and their country have revolted, and \nthey have become pirates like Liamon [Lin Feng] in China. They have \nno employment, except to plunder as much as they can.? The Chinese \nauthorities had already made their own observations about the red-haired \nbarbarians’ (a common and widespread East Asian label referring to the \nDutch) practices offshore and remained on alert. What is noteworthy in the",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1606
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
