      {
        "finding_id": "section-81786a2bce41adf53516",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "rowers attacked coastal towns in Panay, Negros, and Cebu, carrying back \nwith them 800 captive Visayans.\"\"^ Majul summarizes these activities as \nthe first four stages of the “Moro Wars\" (1565-1663), implying that Spanish \nmotivation was fending of piratical incursions into what they perceived \ntheir sphere of influence. In cases when Spanish natives were among the \ncaptives, Muslim negotiators tended to free them upon payment. Majul \nthus countered the narrative of punitive expeditions of the Spaniards \nagainst Muslim piratical actions, providing an important analysis of \nthe complex nature of maritime violence and raiding in Philippine \nwaters following the arrival of the Spaniards.7» More importantly, for \nan understanding of the long-term consequences is Ethan Hawkley's \ndistinguished argument that parallel to Moro-Christian antagonism \nthe early Spanish colonizers relied on Moro intermediation in social, \npolitical, and all above commercial matters.7?",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1663
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
