      {
        "finding_id": "section-f243e0b90f0b2a590210",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The foundations of natural law reasoning lay in an emphasis on the divine \ngift of reason with which humans were to discover universal laws embodying \nboth an imperative for self-preservation and the utility of sociability.” \nThelaus made plain his debt to naturallaw in the introduction to his dis- \nsertation where he argued that because the universal aspiration to friendship \nand peace was regularly distorted by greed and vice, humans could not rely \non reason alone to realize them. Too often, he reasoned, humans became \nfierce beasts to one another. Here, he cited Juvenal's deployment of non- \nhuman animals as moral example: \"When has a stronger lion ever taken a \nweaker lions' life? In what meadow has a boar ever perished by the teeth \nof a bigger boar?\"? Thelaus’ rhetorical strategy here was to emphasize the \nunnaturalness of human unsociability by contrasting it to Juvenal's beasts \nwho were better exemplars of natural fellow feeling. Unlike the beasts, \nhumans were apt to be led astray from reason by their greed and vanity, \nand thus induced to prey upon one another like pirates. In other words, \nThelaus framed piracy as a crime against natural law, human nature, and \ninternational law.?? Having established his moral framework, Thelaus \ndivided the rest of his discussion in De piratica into three parts. In the first, \nthe concept of piracy was defined. The second part focused on assessing \nmoral arguments about pirates and piracy from the standpoint of natural",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 595011,
          "end_char": 596501,
          "start_line": 11258,
          "end_line": 11277,
          "printed_page": null,
          "scan_indexes_0_based": [],
          "pdf_file": null,
          "pdf_reference_base": "LANE_ROOT",
