      {
        "finding_id": "section-a5cc95aa84b74533a031",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "intellectual piracy. Both piracy and philosophy required a life of robbery. \nThough a trite example, Thelaus was prepared to substantiate it by detailing \na number of examples drawn from more sources to show that piracy was not \nonly a common but widely accepted practice in the classical Greek world as \nwell as in Europe's Middle Ages. He cited the example ofthe Vikings, who \nhad practiced piracy and encouraged or enforced it from father to son. The \nNormans also had plundered the seas so successfully the King of France \ngave them part of his realm: Normandy. Plutarch had even claimed that the \nArabs and Spaniards considered piracy as the most beautiful of arts. Thelaus's \nsources on the widespread practice of piracy included Julius Caesar (De Bello \nGallico 6, 3), Aristotle (Politics 1, 5), and the English political philosopher, \nThomas Hobbes (On the Citizen, 5, 2). Hobbes had referred to the ubiquity \nof piracy in human history as evidence for his own controversial argument \nthat the insatiable acquisitiveness of human nature, coupled with constant \nfear, resulted in a war of all against all.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
