      {
        "finding_id": "section-31c1cb857ce2ca73ca8d",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Despite both of them working within the premises of natural law, Thelaus \ntook the time to criticize Hobbes's reasoning. In particular, he denounced \nHobbes's depiction of \"everyone's right to everything\" and “war as is of all \nmen against all men\" as a godless dogma rendered “obsolete by the supreme \nauthorities.\"7 Thelaus's rebuttal of Hobbes was entirely conventional. The \nEnglish philosopher's doctrines were widely attacked by contemporaries \nas leading to atheism and the rejection of a divine basis for earthly power. \nYet, in mounting another attack on Hobbes, Thelaus pivoted his argument \ntowards the need for a more careful moral evaluation of piracy. If Hobbes \nhad been right, and the war of all against and all was universal, then piracy \nwould be no more than expected; nor would there be any reason to suppose \nthat piracy ended when states and empires began. In this respect, Thelaus \nreflected on the famous speech of Calgacus, the leader of last free Celts in \nBritain confronting the armed might of Rome. Calgacus and his speech were \na rhetorical device ofthe Roman senator Tacitus, who invited his readers to \nreflect nostalgically on the heroic Celts. To them, the Romans were merely \n“robbers of the world\" whose “robbery, slaughter, plunder, they give the lying \nname of empire; they make a solitude and call it peace.\" Thelaus's point",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 600710,
