      {
        "finding_id": "section-b1e74fc6fffe65289892",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Thelaus's discussion in this second part ofthe dissertation seemed there- \nfore to have arrived at a paradox. Piracy was both common and an evil, and \npirates even though they masqueraded as monarchs and emperors should be \njudged. That judgment required the invocation of higher standards of moral \nreasoning than human laws alone. Thelaus returned his readers to the moral \nstipulations of natural law and the divine injunction to use reason to promote \nfriendship and sociability. Yet, this rather conventional moral argument, \nstudded with references to a host of well- (and less-) recognized authorities \ndrawn from Europe's classical and more recent history, from Homer to \nGrotius, was merely a prelude to what followed. Here, Thelaus considered \nthe problem of Sweden's policy toward the pirates of the Mediterranean, \nand he therefore confronted once again the uneasy relationship between \nmoral reasoning and political expediency.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
