        "status": "THEMATIC_SOURCE_PASSAGE_NOT_VERIFIED_BASE",
        "lead_type": "SOURCE_SECTION",
        "occurrence_count": 1
      },
      {
        "finding_id": "section-581d6707ba1c55fd7105",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In French Cochinchina, pro-interventionist sentiments were even stronger \nthan in France. In 1882, the Governor of French Cochinchina, Charles Le \nMyre de Vilers, largely on his own accord, but believing that his actions \nwere in accordance with those of the metropolitan government, dispatched \na military expedition to Vietnam. Officially, the purpose was to protect \nthe life and property of French citizens in the country, but covertly the \nintention was to take control over the Red River delta in order to formalize \nand strengthen the implicit French protectorate over Vietnam.” Like ten \nyears earlier, piracy once again figured in the Governor's instructions to \nthe commander of the expedition, Captain Henri Riviére: “You must not \nhave any relations, direct or indirect, with the Black Flags. To us, they are \npirates, and you shall treat them as such [...].\"?",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 665281,
          "end_char": 666153,
          "start_line": 12579,
          "end_line": 12590,
          "printed_page": null,
          "scan_indexes_0_based": [],
          "pdf_file": null,
          "pdf_reference_base": "LANE_ROOT",
          "original_inspected": false
        },
        "reported_cited_pages": [],
        "decision": "REVIEW_CONTEXT",
        "reason": "THEMATIC_SOURCE_PASSAGE_NOT_VERIFIED_BASE",
        "extraction_method": "DETERMINISTIC_THEMATIC_PASSAGE_READING",
        "uncertainties": [
          "ORIGINAL_NOT_INSPECTED",
          "SOURCE_GENEALOGY_PENDING",
          "INDEPENDENCE_UNRESOLVED",
          "TOPONYM_AND_USAGE_REQUIRE_SEMANTIC_EXTRACTION",
          "DATE_OUTSIDE_SCOPE",
          "REGIONAL_SCOPE_REQUIRES_REVIEW"
        ],
        "evidence_state": "ACQUIRED",
        "original_inspected": false,
        "verified": false,
        "status": "THEMATIC_SOURCE_PASSAGE_NOT_VERIFIED_BASE",
        "lead_type": "SOURCE_SECTION",
        "occurrence_count": 1
      },
      {
        "finding_id": "section-5136b0ba37714870c5bc",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The French troops - who were more numerous and better equipped than \nGarnier's force ten years earlier — quickly seized the citadel at Hanoi but \nwere again unable to move against the Black Flags. Riviére was also unable \nto undertake an intended survey of the Red River because the water was too \nlow for the French gunboats. Forced to wait for the rain season the French \ntroops were thus confined to the citadel, where they were besieged by the \nBlack Flags. An obviously despondent Riviére wrote in a letter to one of his \nsub commanders that the country seemed to be teeming with pirates and \nthat more or less everybody was a pirate.",
