      {
        "finding_id": "section-4faec3d5bb6d7f298a1b",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The  quality  which  Napoleon  is  said  to  have \nascribed  to  the  British  Infantry,  \"  of  never  knowing \nwhen  they  were  beaten/'  seems  to  have  also  char- \nacterised the  Sea-wolves  ;  as  witness  the  marvellous \nrecuperation  of  Kheyr-ed-Din  Barbarossa  when \nexpelled  from  Tunis  by  Charles  V.  ;  and  the  escape \nof  Dragut  from  the  island  of  Jerba  when  apparently \nhopelessly  trapped  by  the  Genoese  admiral,  Andrea \nDoria.  All  through  their  history  the  leaders  of  the \nSea-wolves  show  the  resourcefulness  of  the  real  sea- \nmen that  they  had  become  by  force  of  circumstances, \nand  it  was  they  who  in  the  age  in  which  they  dwelt \nshowed  what  sea  power  really  meant.  Sailing  through \nthe  Mediterranean  on  my  way  to  Malta  in  the  spring \nof  this  year,  as  the  good  ship  fared  onwards  I  passed \nin  succession  all  those  lurking-places  from  which  the \nMoslem  Corsairs  were  wont  to  burst  out  upon  their \nprey.  Truly  it  seemed  as  if",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
