      {
        "finding_id": "section-9e4acccf3e884a19e0af",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "To  the  peaceful  mariner  who  wished  merely  to \ntrade,  to  the  individual  whose  business  called  him \noverseas,  this  epoch  must  have  been  one  of  terror \nunspeakable.  The  ordinary  perils  of  the  deep  were \nquite  enough  to  keep  timid  folk  at  home  in  those \ndays  of  clumsy,  ill-found  sailing  ships,  which  could \nby  no  means  work  to  windward,  and  did  not  sail \nremarkably  well  even  with  the  most  favouring \nbreezes ;  when  to  this  we  add  that  every  ship \nwhich  started  on  a  voyage  in  the  Mediterranean \nhad  before  her  the  chance  of  being  captured  by  the \ncorsairs,  it  was  no  wonder  that  he  whose  business \nled  him  oversea  should  make  his  last  will  and  testa- \nment and  bid  a  fond  farewell  to  all  his  relatives.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
