      {
        "finding_id": "section-9e1ac51132325a795074",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "the  tribesmen  were  still  his  very  faithful  friends \nand  allies.  The  corsair  was  more  patient  than  his \nwont.  In  this  affair  he  wished  for  ample  proof  of \ndelinquency,  and  also  for  a  vengeance  adequate  to \nthe  occasion  when  he  should  discover  all  the  guilty \nparties  ;  and  so  some  weeks  went  by  while  the  plot \nwas  maturing,  apparently,  from  the  point  of  view \nof  the  conspirators,  to  a  successful  conclusion. \nBut  Uruj  had  bided  his  time  with  a  subtlety  and \nfinesse  which  would  have  done  credit  to  Kheyr-ed- \nDin  himself.",
