      {
        "finding_id": "section-2157d15ec28909fca4a1",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "complaints,  there  were  loud  and  continuous  ones \nconcerning  the  corsairs.  Venice,  \"  The  Bride  of \nthe  Sea,\"  had  neither  rest  nor  peace ;  the  pirates \nswarmed  in  Corfu,  in  Zante,  in  Candia,  in  Cepha- \nlonia,  and  the  plunder  and  murder  of  the  subjects \nof  the  Republic  was  the  theme  of  perpetual  repre- \nsentations to  the  Sultan.  The  balance  of  advantage \nin  this  guerilla  warfare  was  with  the  corsairs  until \nGirolame  Canale,  a  Venetian  captain,  seized  one  of \nthe  Moslem  leaders  known  as  \"  The  Young  Moor  of \nAlexandria.\"  The  victory  of  Canale  was  some- \nwhat an  important  one,  as  he  captured  the  galley \nof  \"  The  Young  Moor  \"  and  four  others  ;  two  more \nwere  sunk,  and  three  hundred  Janissaries  and  one \nthousand  slaves  fell  into  the  hands  of  the  Venetian \ncommander.  There  being  an  absence  of  nice  feeling \non  the  part  of  the  Venetians,  the  Janissaries  were  at \nonce  beheaded  to  a  man.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
