      {
        "finding_id": "section-2d11a65122b081e12a21",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "thousand  men.  Sinan  Reis,  an  old  Osmanli  warrior, \nfurious  with  jealousy  that  the  chief  command \nshould  be  in  the  hands  of  a  corsair,  sustained  his \nopinion  in  a  manner  which  augured  ill  for  the \nhearty  co-operation  of  all  the  Turkish  forces. \nSinan  was  just  one  of  those  blindly  valiant  fighters \nfrom  whom  the  politic  Ibrahim  had  desired  to  deliver \nhis  master  when  he  had  urged  the  appointment \nof  Kheyr-ed-Din  :  brave  as  a  lion,  keen  as  the  edge \nof  his  own  good  scimitar,  fanatical,  as  became  a \nHodja  who  had  visited  the  Holy  Places,  Sinan  was \na  type  of  the  Turkish  sea-officer :  devoid  of  strate- \ngical instinct  and  tactical  training,  his  one  idea  was \na  headlong  attack,  then  victory  or  the  houris  of \nParadise.  It  will  be  seen  that  Barbarossa  had  not \nonly  Doria  and  the  Christian  fleet  and  army  against \nwhich  to  contend  on  this  occasion.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
