      {
        "finding_id": "section-dc0e3ba7e64d2889cb2b",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "consequence  refused  to  listen  to  the  frenzied  appeals \nof  the  excited  Moslems  to  be  led  against  the  Chris- \ntian dogs.  It  may  seem  a  contradiction  in  terms  to \nspeak  of  the  moral  courage  of  a  pirate  ;  but  if  ever \nthat  quality  were  displayed  to  its  fullest  extent  it  was \nexhibited  by  Barbarossa  in  the  Prevesa  campaign. \nIn  his  intellectual  outlook  on  all  that  was  passing, \nboth  inside  and  outside  of  the  Gulf  of  Arta,  in  this \nSeptember  of  1538,  we  see  Kheyr-ed-Din  at  his  best. \nEver  a  fighter,  he  knew  when  to  give  battle  and  when \nto  refrain,  when  to  sweep  headlong  upon  the  foe,  but \nalso  when  to  hold  back  and  to  baffle  by  waiting  till \nthe  psychological  moment  should  arrive.  Around  him \nSinan-Reis,  Mourad-Reis,  and  half  a  hundred  others \nof  their  kidney  were  clamouring  ;  they  hurled  insults \nat  his  head,  they  heaped  opprobrium  on  \"  the \ncorsair,\"  they  practically  incited  their  troops  to \nmutiny  in  their  mad  appeals  to  be  led  against  the \nfoe.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
