      {
        "finding_id": "section-d7784b3748a516df4f5d",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "the  Moslems  were  apt  to  take  to  the  sea  in  a  private \ncapacity  ;  a  certain  number  of  them  joining  together \nto  man  a  small  craft  which  was  known  as  a  brigan- \ntine.  As  has  been  said  in  a  previous  chapter,  this \nword  must  not  be  understood  in  the  light  of  the \nterminology  of  the  modern  seaman  :  the  brigantines \nof  the  Moslem  corsairs  were  really  large  rowing \nboats,  carrying  fourteen  to  twenty-six  oars,  and \nmade  as  seaworthy  as  the  small  size  of  such  craft \nwould  allow.  Should  the  venture  of  the  crew  of \na  brigantine  prove  successful,  then  the  reis,  or \ncaptain,  might  blossom  out  into  the  command  of \na  galley,  hi  which  his  oars  would  be  manned  by \nhis  slaves ;  but,  in  the  first  instance,  he  would  man \nhis  brigantine  with  a  crew  of  Moslem  desperadoes \nworking  on  the  share  system  and  dividing  anything \nthat  they  could  pick  up ;  in  this  manner  most  of \nthose  corsairs  who  became  famous  commenced  their \ncareers,  and  rose  as  we  have  seen  from  the  thwart \nof  a  brigantine  to  the  unstable  eminence  of  a  throne \nin  Algiers,  Tunis,  or  Tlemcen.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
