      {
        "finding_id": "section-dd47da657593d3a38303",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "between 1500 and 1900. The first of these is piracy in international law and \npolitics. The origins of modern international law are frequently sought in \nthe Early Modern period, and piracy has often been accorded a major role \nin this development, as well as in the emergence of an international system \nof states. In his contribution to this section, Michael Kempe highlights how \ninternational law developed through a process that he calls “integration by \nexclusion.\" Specifically, he focuses on the piratical exploits and subsequent \ntrial of John Cusack, an Irish privateer-turned-pirate who was sentenced to \ndeath by the Admiralty in England and executed in 1675. The case illustrates \nhow accusations of piracy as a crime against all nations was a central element \nin the emergence of international law in Europe and in the establishment \nof England's claim to be an effective global sea power. Kempe also argues \nthat the sentence was meant to demonstrate to other European nations that \nEngland was willing and able to project its jurisdiction at sea far beyond \nthe country's shorelines.",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1675
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
