      {
        "finding_id": "section-c7224084c17f0499729b",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Ihave argued in this chapter for a slightly different view. In effect, Locke's \n(and Tindal's) *pyrates\" served a more ambivalent purpose, to position not \nonly the maritime marauder, but also the terrestrial tyrant (James IT) as \nthe common pest of humankind. Piracy, for them, was not simply a matter \nof legal definition, but of moral and political argument about the basis \nof political order and the legitimacy of sovereignty. In this construction, \nthe tyrant and the pirate symbolized the inevitable insecurity that lay \nbeyond the bounds ofcivil society, in which legal restrictions on the use of \nviolence gained no purchase. The insecurity they embodied was twofold; \neach was to blame for the insecurity born of their own violence, but each \nwas also subject to the constant fear of becoming victims to the unrestrained \nviolence of another in the state of nature. It was this same doubled insecurity \nthat Captain Johnson imagined in the opening biography of his General \nHistory: the \"Life of Captain Avery.\"7 As depicted here, Avery's crew found \nthemselves cut adrift on the cruel seas of the state of nature where their \nonly security lay in their own power of self-defence, constantly excited by \nmutual jealously and suspicion among themselves. Johnson's tale of Avery's \ncrew was far-removed from the picaresque fantasy of the \"successful pirate,\" \nand was, in fact, a ready-made rebuttal of the contractual argument for \ngovernment by consent that had been mobilized by Locke and others to \nlegitimate the (still relatively) new Whiggish political order established \nin 1688. In Johnson's General History, Avery's crews' fate illustrated why \nthe contractual and consensual order of pirate articles led straight to the \nnightmare of ravenous insecurity, perpetual vigilance, and ineradicable \nviolence. It is telling that Johnson could find no better comparison to \ndescribe their situation than to consider them all as petty “tyrants,” for \neach of whom:",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1688
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
