      {
        "finding_id": "section-8b5ac707eb8a1cd571b4",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "small-scale operations to massive land raids involving large ships with over \na hundred crew, and sometimes whole fleets.’ By 1700, the first reports \nwere recorded of pirates flying the Jolly Roger (by captain Emanuel Wynn), \nthe infamous black flag that soon came to signify pirate identity. By the end \nofthe War of Spanish Succession (1701-1713), piracy reemerged. In 1716, the \nyear Thelaus published his dissertation, Caribbean pirate activity reached \npreviously unknown heights of intensity emanating from headquarters \non New Providence in the Bahamas.? Already European pirate crews, \nestimated to number more than a thousand, were hunting in the Indian \nOcean, setting up bases on the northeast coast of Madagascar and on the \nislet of Santa-Maria.? Some among them petitioned Sweden in May 1714 \nfor protection against persecution. Through the Swedish ambassador in \nHanover, a messenger was sent to ask for king Karl XII's clemency.'? The \npirates argued that there were not many Swedes among them, and that \nthey had never attacked Swedish vessels. Negotiations rapidly stalled until \nKaspar Wilhelm Morgan, who presented himself as a pirate captain, was \nsent directly to Strómstad in Sweden with a petition to Karl XII in early \nJune 1718. Morgan offered the king riches collected by the pirates, as well \nas the use of their ships, and a colony they had founded on Madagascar. \nIn return, the pirates asked for asylum and to become Swedish subjects. \nRemarkably, Karl agreed to these terms on 24 June 1718.\"",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1700
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
