        "source_text_sha256": "19c55bfa97af33a51960ccd4a626fb5f63bf82a265383326891077fccf9de0a0",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The Knights' corsairing activity was equally carried\nout in the Levant, particularly in order to intercept the\nrich traffic proceeding from Alexandria and other\nports of the Near East towards Constantinople. Pierre\nRocqueluare de Saint Aubain and Marthurino de\nLescaut, known as Romegas, were among those who\ndistinguished themselves in such operations. De Saint\nAubain was a knight-corsair who in 1566 managed\n10 capture a very precious cargo of spices and some\nninety slaves from captured Turkish vessels. 19\nRomegas had a series of victories starting in the late\n1550s. In 1557, he captured, among others, two heavy\nTurkish merchant ships in the Levant. ,0 In 1561,\nRomegas managed to capture 300 slaves and several\nrich cargoes at the mouth of the Nile. In 1563 having\nset out with two galIeys, 21 he was sailing back to\nCapo Passero with over 500 slaves and heaped On two\nships the cargoes of eight ships he had captured. In",
