      {
        "finding_id": "section-f11b9861f20edafd4fb4",
        "file": "893cb321e932ba3a878252fb.txt",
        "title": "Images of the Mediterranean in an Ottoman Pirate Novel from the Late Seventeenth Century",
        "url": "https://makale.isam.org.tr/server/api/core/bitstreams/e9b22c6b-e4de-482a-be2e-33b284a17014/content",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": null,
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "9348e8969051079250376bb40ba76a85a2807ca05ec788735fb51803f5573fd4",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Images of the Mediterranean\nHowever, in the rest of the paper I will try to focus on the image of the Eastern\nMediterranean among Ottoman seamen, as illustrated in the text. In other words,\nwhat type of ‘cognitive / mental map’ would a sailor use in order to represent a sea\nhe certainly knew, with its ports, established itineraries, and known or unknown\nwaters?\nA first observation we have to make is that the text does not mention any\nmaps whatsoever. However, this does not mean that navigation was just an\nempirical matter of experience. For example, when describing a storm during which the compass of the ship was lost, the author states that everything\nseemed to be lost, since “compass is the eye of the ship” (zira pusula sefînenin\nChrysostomides, Ch. Dendrinos, and J. Harris (Camberley: Porphyrogenitus, 2004) [also published in eadem, Studies in Pre-Ottoman Turkey and the Ottomans, Variorum Collected Studies\n(Aldershot: Ashgate, 2007)], 199-212, esp. 209ff.\n21 Bennassar and Bennassar, Les chrétiens d’Allah, 215-216 and passim; Evliya Çelebi, Evliya Çelebi\nSeyahatnâmesi: Topkapî Sarayı Kütüphanesi Bağdat 308 Numaralı Yazmanın Transkripsyonu –\nDizini, vol. 8, eds. Seyit Ali Kahraman, Yücel Dağlı, Robert Dankoff (Istanbul: Yapı Kredi\nYayınları, 2003), 282, states that the corsairs of Aya Mavra (Santa Maura, Leukada) could speak\nTurkish, Greek or “Frankish”: lisânları Urûmca çalup fasîh ü belîğ Rûm lisânı ve Freng zebânı\ntekellüm ederler; Urûmca stands for the Balkan Turkish dialects, see op.cit., 89-90, 94, 127-28;\nand Robert Dankoff, From Mahmud Kaşgari to Evliya Çelebi: Studies in Middle Turkic and Ottoman Literatures (Istanbul: Isis Press, 2008), 276.\n22 Kafadar, Between Two Worlds, 83.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
