        },
        "source_text_sha256": "1901bb10294e81f32d98c63cba8e03792d5ae5388c5c8aacfc5b904d67fab94d",
        "evidence_excerpt": "At this time the power of the pirates of the Mediter- \nranean was at its height. No country upon the shores \nof that sea was safe from their depredations for many \nmiles inland. Ever since history began pirates had \nranged the Middle Sea, but for some forty years special \ncircumstances had afforded them the means of increasing \ntheir influence. The conquest of Greece by the Romans \nhad thrown thousands of dispossessed and discontented \nGreeks into the ranks of the sea-robbers ; then had come \nthe civil wars in Rome, which had caused the Romans to \noverlook events passing on the sea ; and, finally, the aid \ngiven by the pirates to Mithridates, King of Pontus, in \nhis long war with the Roman State had increased their \nconfidence and daring.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/bookofpirates0000gilb",
