      {
        "finding_id": "section-bf52fe554824d27320a0",
        "file": "bookofpirates0000gilb_bookofpirates0000gilb_djvu.txt",
        "title": "The book of pirates",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/bookofpirates0000gilb",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Gilbert, Henry, 1868-1937",
          "date": [
            "1916-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "1901bb10294e81f32d98c63cba8e03792d5ae5388c5c8aacfc5b904d67fab94d",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Then, having dressed some of his men in the garb of \nthe dead pirates, he had placed them so as to deceive \nthe main body of Saxons when they should return. Ina \nstorm of wind and rain, indeed, they were soon heard \ncoming back, the booty. from two or three villas slung \nover their backs, but they had no captives with them, as \nthey were moving quickly. They got almost to the \nboats before they suspected something was wrong, but \nthen it was too late. A shrill trumpet sounded and the \nRoman soldiers, closing in upon them, overwhelmed and \nslew them all, not one escaping. Ever since then Wulf, \nthe little Saxon boy, whose father had been slain with the \nothers, had been kept by Cadgan, and after a time had \nseemed to forget his hatred of his captors and had become \nalmost like a brother to Elphin.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
