      {
        "finding_id": "section-4f5619836de70b2b7756",
        "file": "bookofpirates0000gilb_bookofpirates0000gilb_djvu.txt",
        "title": "The book of pirates",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/bookofpirates0000gilb",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Gilbert, Henry, 1868-1937",
          "date": [
            "1916-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "1901bb10294e81f32d98c63cba8e03792d5ae5388c5c8aacfc5b904d67fab94d",
        "evidence_excerpt": "It was a hundred years before, about the year 260, \nthat the long low ships of the sea-rovers had first begun \nto be seen and feared on the coasts of Britain and Gaul \nalong the Narrow Seas. For a few years little notice was \ntaken of them in either province, but then the complaints \nof townsmen in the coast towns, or of well-to-do dwellers in \ncountry villas, and of villagers became so bitter that the \nRoman governors had to do something to cope with the \nnuisance. In Britain, therefore, a special leader was \nappointed, generally a man who had been a good seaman, \nand to his charge was committed the defence of the shores \nwhich the pirates infested. Certain coast forts were \nbuilt and strongly defended, and in these were stationed \nthe troops whose duty it was to assist in beating off the \npirate bands. A fleet of galleys was built, to act as \nscouts upon the lonely seas, and to bring word swiftly \nback when they sighted the squadron of pirate craft \nmaking toward Britain or Gaul over the leaping waves. \nWhen news came that the pirates were coming the trumpet \nrang out, and the seamen and fighting men manned the \nwar-galleys, which put out to sea to meet the pirate fleet \nand to do battle with it. On shore horsemen were sent \nswiftly from one fort or station to another to put the \nlegionary officers on guard, so that if by chance the \npirates broke through the Roman fleet or defeated it \nthey would still be prevented from landing or they would",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/bookofpirates0000gilb",
          "source_file": "bookofpirates0000gilb_bookofpirates0000gilb_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 85797,
          "end_char": 87265,
          "start_line": 2470,
          "end_line": 2495,
          "printed_page": null,
