      {
        "finding_id": "section-e409cea41ba85c57e35f",
        "file": "bookofpirates0000gilb_bookofpirates0000gilb_djvu.txt",
        "title": "The book of pirates",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/bookofpirates0000gilb",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Gilbert, Henry, 1868-1937",
          "date": [
            "1916-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "1901bb10294e81f32d98c63cba8e03792d5ae5388c5c8aacfc5b904d67fab94d",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Sailing-ships did not require hundreds of slaves for | \ntheir crews, but nevertheless the number of captives \ntaken did not diminish. There was naturally no lack \nof employment to which they could be put, on shore, \nand the demand was always equal to the supply. Ac- \ncording to the character of their masters they were \ntreated with brutality or humanity, but the lot of the \nmajority was wretched in the extreme. The Christian \nnations, instead of combining to extirpate the whole \nof the evil traffickers and pirates, were accustomed to \nsend consuls to reside at Algiers and Tunis to protect \ntheir miserable compatriots as much as possible and to \nnegotiate ransoms for those whose friends could afford \nto pay them. Charitable bodies were formed in Christian \ncountries to collect funds wherewith to ransom poor \ncaptives, the most famous of these being the French \norder of priesthood known as the Redemptionist Fathers. \nThese priests, clad in white robes with a cross in blue \nand red upon the breast, went boldly to the strongholds \nof the brutal pirates and bargained for the release of \nFrench slaves.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
