      {
        "finding_id": "section-a4ac08b4e872bbe163e2",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "That  a  pirate  should  be  a  warrior  goes  without \nsaying,  that  a  pirate  should  be  a  statesman  is  a \nthing  almost  incredible ;  but  those  who  will  read \nthe  story  of  the  life  of  Kheyr-ed-Din  Barbarossa \nwill  be  forced  to  admit  that  here,  at  least,  was  a \npirate  who  achieved  the  apparently  impossible. \nAdmiral  Jurien  de  la  Graviere  has  remarked  that \nthe  Moslem  corsairs  of  the  sixteenth  century  were \ngreat  men,  even  when  measured  by  the  standard \nof  Henry  VIII.,  of  Charles  V.,  of  Soliman  the \nMagnificent,  of  Ibrahim,  his  Grand  Vizier,  or  of \nAndrea  Doria,  greatest  among  contemporary \nChristian  mariners.  To  the  seaman,  of  course, \nthere  is  much  that  is  fascinating  in  the  deeds  of",
        "years_in_scope": [],
