      {
        "finding_id": "section-5448a3ce8c9b9db3e4c1",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "This  time  he  presented  himself  to  Khorkud,  the \nGovernor  of  Caramania,  brother  to  Sultan  Selim, \nthe  Grand  Turk.  The  Governor,  recognising  him \nas  an  intrepid  mariner,  ordered  the  Basha  of  Smyrna \nto  furnish  him  with  a  ship  fitted  for  that  guerre  de \ncourse,  which  he  desired  to  pursue  against  the \nChristians.  The  value  of  the  corsair  as  an  auxiliary \nwas  beginning  to  be  recognised  among  the  high \nTurkish  officials.  For  the  complaisance  of  Khorkud \nthere  were  two  reasons  :  in  the  first  place,  he  was \nacting  in  the  interests  of  his  brother  in  sending  to \nsea  any  really  capable  man  to  make  head  against \nhis  enemies,  and  the  fact  that  Uruj  was  a  pirate \npure  and  simple  did  not  weigh  for  a  feather  in  the \nbalance  ;  in  the  second  place,  it  was  a  decidedly \ngood  mercantile  speculation  as  he  ordered  his  in- \nferior, the  Basha  of  Egypt,  to  bear  the  expense  of \nfitting  out  the  necessary  ship — which  came  to  some \n5,000  ducats — and  doubtless  received  a  handsome \npercentage  on  all  captures  from  his  grateful  prot^g^.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
