          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "It  may  have  been  that  the  Emperor  was  dis- \npleased with  this  excess  of  zeal  on  the  part  of  his \narmy  ;  but,  if  it  were  so,  the  chroniclers  are  silent \nconcerning  the  matter,  being  far  too  busy  singing \nthe  praises  of  the  Caesar  to  think  of  such  a  trifle  as \nthe  massacre  of  most  of  the  persons  whom  he  had \ncome  to  deliver.  The  wretched  inhabitants  of \nTunis  must  have  found  it  somewhat  difficult  to \ndistinguish  between  the  corsair,  who  killed  three \nthousand  of  their  fellow  townsmen,  and  the  Christian \nEmperor,  who  had  massacred  ten  times  that \nnumber.  Charles,  however,  reaped  great  glory \nfrom  an  expedition  which  had  but  one  good  result, \nwhich  was,  that  he  succeeded  in  rescuing  twenty \nthousand  captives ;  these  men,  very  naturally,  on",
