      {
        "finding_id": "section-70b390457b417a1c3b69",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "\"  During  some  years,\"  says  J.  Morgan  in  his \nCompleat  History  of  Algiers,  1728,  \"  he  was  by \nthat  basha  intrusted  in  the  direction  of  sundry \nmomentous  expeditions ;  in  which  he  acquitted \nhimself  much  to  the  satisfaction  of  his  principal : \nas  having  never  once  been  unsuccessful.\"  When  we \nremember  the  treatment  meted  out  by  Barbarossa \nto  some  of  his  unsuccessful  lieutenants,  Dragut \nmust  be  esteemed  a  very  fortunate  man.  His \nmaster,  we  are  told,  advanced  him  to  all  the \nmihtary  offices  of  the  State — it  would  be  interesting \nto  know  what  these  were  in  a  purely  piratical  con- \nfederation ruled  by  a  pirate  !  In  the  end  Dragut \nwas  appointed  to  be  kayia,  or  lieutenant,  and \ngiven  entire  command  of  twelve  galleys.",
