      {
        "finding_id": "section-cbfd57d0b9f61bd0f20b",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Charles  V.  had  \"  smoked  out  the  fox,\"  but  his \nadmiral  in  so  doing  had  not  succeeded  in  capturing \nthat  remarkably  wily  animal ;  for  Dragut  was  not \nonly  stiU  at  liberty,  but  was  burning  for  revenge \non  those  by  whom  he  had  been  dispossessed.  He \nhad  lost  \"  his  city,\"  as  he  called  \"  Africa  \"  ;  he  had \nlost  two  thousand  five  hundred  men — among  them \nsome  of  the  fiercest  and  most  experienced  of  his \ncorsairs  ;  he  had  lost  ten  thousand  slaves,  repre- \nsenting a  large  sum  of  money,  and  much  wealth \nbesides.  The  corsair,  however,  was  not  one  of \nthose  who  merely  sit  down  and  repine ;  for  him \nstrenuous  and  continued  action  was  the  law  of  his \nbeing,  and  he  at  once  repaired  to  Constantinople. \nHere  he  was  well  known  as  an  adroit  and  skilful \nseaman  and  a  most  determined  enemy  of  the \nChristians,  and,  in  consequence,  was  not  only \ncertain  of  a  welcome,  but  of  substantial  help  as \nwell,  if  he  could  but  win  over  the  Grand  Turk  to \ntake   the  same  view  of  his  grievances  as  he  did",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
