      {
        "finding_id": "section-69e4ce609f0557dbef4a",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "his  armadas,  who  let  slip  no  opportunity  of  harrying \nthe  Moslem  wheresoever  he  was  to  be  found.  In \nthis  there  was  more  than  a  grain  of  truth,  as  we \nshall  see  when  we  come  to  the  next  chapter,  which \nwill  be  devoted  to  a  sketch  of  this  militant  order. \nSuffice  it  to  say  here  that  the  Knights  fought  for \nwhat  they  termed  \"  the  Religion \"  (it  was  in  this \nmanner  they  designated  their  confederacy),  and  to \nharry  and  enslave  the  Mussulman,  to  destroy  him \nas  a  noxious  animal  wherever  he  was  to  be  found, \nwas  the  reason  for  which  they  existed.  It  is  true \nthat  they  plundered  not  for  individual  gain,  but \nmany  was  the  rich  prize  towed  into  Malta  past \nSt.  Elmo  and  the  ominously  named  \"  Punta \ndelle  Forche  \"  (the  \"  Point  of  the  Gallows,\"  where \nall  captured  pirates  were  hanged),  the  proceeds  of \nwhich  went  to  the  enrichment  of  the  Order ;  to \nbuy  themselves  the  wherewithal  to  fight  with  the \nMahommedan  again.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
