      {
        "finding_id": "section-d32fab58a3b5c222a336",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "which  was  permitted  by  the  terrain,  or  that  the \nart  of  fortification  admitted  at  this  epoch.  The \ncastle,  which  was  not  advantageously  placed,  was, \nnotwithstanding,  put  in  a  state  of  defence  by  an \nenormous  expenditure  of  money.  Great  towers,  in \nwhich  were  mounted  many  big  guns,  defended  the \nentrance  to  the  port,  which  had  become  the  head- \nquarters of  the  vessels  owned  by  Dragut,  and  also \nof  those  corsairs  who  sailed  their  craft  under  the \ncrescent  flag  of  the  Sultan  of  Constantinople.  It \nwas  against  such  a  fortress  as  this  that  the  Duke \nof  Medina- Cell  went  up  :  we  have  no  space  to  deal \nhere  with  the  details  of  this  attack,  which  ended \nin  the  hopeless  and  irremediable  defeat  of  the \nChristian  forces.  The  Duke  was  an  incompetent \ncommander  ;  he  was  opposed  to  one  of  the  greatest \nleaders  of  the  age— an  expert  in  almost  every \nbranch  of  the  science  of  war,  in  command  of  a \nlarge  body  of  the  fiercest  fighters  of  the  day,  who \never  feared  the  wrath  of  Dragut  more  than  the \nswords  of  the  enemy.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
