      {
        "finding_id": "section-2efe8a3feeb72ac28d27",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Those  of  them  who  were  in  Algiers  demanded \n\"  Who  was  this  corsair  who  dared   to   keep  the \npicked  men  of  the  army  of  the  Grand  Turk,  waiting \nfor  their  pay,  as  if  they  were  no  better  than  his \nslaves  ? \"     Such  a  thing  as  a  mutiny  was,  in  the \ndays  of  which  we  speak,  a  matter  for  which  any \nprudent  corsair  had  to  be  prepared.     Ali  was  in \nno  means  discomposed,  and,  as  the  crisis  had  be- \ncome acute  on  shore,  he  went  to  sea,   where  he \nwas  under  no  obligation  to  pay  his  men,  who  paid \nthemselves  at  the  expense  of  their  enemies.     He \nput  to  sea  with  twenty  galleys,  and,  shortly  after \nleaving   Algiers,  he  met  with  a  galley  from  the \nLevant,  from  which  he  received  information  that \na  powerful  armada  was  preparing  in  Constantinople \nfor  an   expedition    against   the    Christians.      He \nsteered  for  Coron  in   the   Morea,  where   he   was \nalmost  immediately  joined  by  the  Ottoman  fleet, \nthe  commander  of  which  force  was  overjoyed  to",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
