      {
        "finding_id": "section-81e55bece3216b01c47d",
        "file": "cu31924087978445_cu31924087978445_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean; the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924087978445",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton (Edward Hamilton)",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e53211270a60a26aa03dc5168c85f7a9043def78664e708f0be035cea4a28c60",
        "evidence_excerpt": "When  we  of  the  present  day  read  of  the  deeds \nof  the  corsairs  we  are  filled  with  horror,  we  fail \nto  understand  how  such  things  could  have  been \ntolerated,  we  seek  for  some  explanation.  When \nwe  hear  of  a  \"  League  of  Christian  Princes,\"  and \nfind  that  all  its  members  could  accomphsh  was  to \nturn  their  arms  the  one  against  the  other,  we  are \neven  still  more  puzzled.  What  was  it,  then,  that \nlay  at  the  root  of  this  problem  ?  The  answer \nwould  appear  to  be  in  the  ethical  standpoint  of \nthe  sixteenth  century.  We  are  so  accustomed  in \nthe  present  day  to  hear  of  the  rights  of  man  that \nwe  are  apt  to  forget  that,  in  the  time  of  Barbarossa, \nof  Dragut,  of  Charles  V.,  and  the  Medicean \nPopes  such  a  thing  did  not  exist,  and  the  only \nrights  possessed  by  the  common  man  were  those",
        "years_in_scope": [],
